// Neo4j Backup — Document-Nodes (ohne Embeddings)
// Anzahl: 1320 Nodes

MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Affect Voice Lifetime', content: '**Affect Voice Lifetime** [modulation] A parameter in certain Grid modules that determines whether the module influences how long a voice is kept active. Wann: To control the duration of a voice based on envelope stages, note gate signals, or audio levels.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Amplitude Envelope Generator (AEG)', content: '**Amplitude Envelope Generator (AEG)** [modulation] A modulation source that controls the amplitude of the sound over time, with Attack, Decay, Sustain, and Release controls. Wann: To shape the attack and decay of a sound, creating dynamic and expressive performances.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Analysis', content: '**Analysis** [device_category] Devices that visualize signals — Oscilloscope, Spectrum. Make no effect on audio chain. Wann: Monitoring signal levels, frequencies, and waveforms during mixing.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Arrange View', content: '**Arrange View** [navigation] The primary view for assembling music by recording and ordering clips. Arranger Timeline Panel is central. Shows all project tracks together. Wann: Composing and arranging full songs, viewing the overall structure, recording automation.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Arranger', content: '**Arranger** [arrangement] Provides settings that apply when working within the Arranger Timeline Panel. Wann: When arranging and editing clips in the Arranger Timeline Panel.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Arranger Clips', content: '**Arranger Clips** [arrangement] Clips are the smallest unit of musical ideas in Bitwig Studio, used for arranging tasks. They can be thought of as musical atoms and are the foundation of music creation in Bitwig. Wann: When creating and arranging music in the Arranger, clips are used to build compositions by looping and manipulating musical ideas.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Arranger Loop', content: '**Arranger Loop** [arrangement] A toggle that enables a looped section of the timeline for recording, allowing for comping or repeated recording within a defined range. Wann: When you want to record multiple takes of audio or MIDI within a specific looped section for comping or refinement.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Arranger Loop Selector', content: '**Arranger Loop Selector** [navigation] A tool that defines the region of the Arranger Timeline that will be looped during playback. Wann: To create a repeating section of the Arranger Timeline for continuous playback.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Arranger Timeline', content: '**Arranger Timeline** [arrangement] Linear sequencer operating across a standard musical timeline. The primary place for sketching and producing full-length songs. Clips placed here follow a fixed timeline. Wann: Composing full songs with defined structure — verse, chorus, bridge. Recording automation over time. Final arrangement before export.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Arranger Timeline Panel', content: '**Arranger Timeline Panel** [navigation] Allows you to see all of your project\'s tracks, create an arrangement with timeline clips, and edit track automation. Wann: When arranging and editing your project\'s timeline and automation.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Audio FX', content: '**Audio FX** [device_category] Manipulate incoming audio signals. Examples: Blur, Freq Shifter, Ring-Mod, Treemonster. Wann: Adding texture, effects, and character to audio signals.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Audio Thru', content: '**Audio Thru** [modulation] A parameter that allows Audio Grid devices to pass through audio signals to the output. Wann: To maintain audio signals when adding processing or modulation to a patch.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Automation Editor Panel', content: '**Automation Editor Panel** [automation] Gives detailed control over track automation, clip automation, and MIDI control messages. Central to the Edit View alongside the Detail Editor. Wann: Drawing and editing automation curves for any parameter over time. Creating filter sweeps, volume fades, effect changes.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Beat Grid', content: '**Beat Grid** [navigation] A system that defines the rhythmic divisions in the Arranger Timeline, allowing for precise alignment of clips and events. Wann: Used to align clips and events with specific rhythmic divisions for precise arrangement and editing.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Big Meters Section', content: '**Big Meters Section** [mixing] High-resolution stereo audio meters that display the output level of each channel. Wann: To monitor and ensure proper levels for each track.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Browser', content: '**Browser** [browser] A tool for searching and accessing content such as sources, collections, creators, tags, and more within Bitwig Studio. Wann: To quickly find and load audio sources, instruments, effects, or other content into your project.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Browser Panel', content: '**Browser Panel** [browser] Allows previewing, loading, saving, and tagging content from the Bitwig Studio library. Used to find and load devices, presets, samples, and projects. Wann: Finding instruments and FX by category, searching by name or tag, previewing sounds before loading.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Browser Snapshots', content: '**Browser Snapshots** [browser] A saved set of search configurations in the Bitwig browser, allowing users to quickly recall specific filter settings. Wann: To quickly return to a specific search configuration without reapplying filters manually.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Category filter', content: '**Category filter** [browser] A filter that allows users to limit search results to a specific category. Wann: Use to narrow down results based on categories like \'Drum Kits\' or \'Synth Presets\'.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Channels filter', content: '**Channels filter** [browser] A filter that appears when Impulses are selected, allowing users to limit results to files with a specific number of audio channels. Wann: Use when searching for audio files with a specific channel count, such as stereo or mono.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Choke Group', content: '**Choke Group** [device] A feature that allows one chain to silence another when triggered, useful for managing overlapping sounds like hi-hats. Wann: To ensure only one sound plays at a time within a group, such as muting an open hi-hat when a closed hi-hat is triggered.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Clip', content: '**Clip** [arrangement] Container for individual musical ideas. Clips store either notes or audio, as well as control and automation data. Wann: Storing MIDI note patterns or audio recordings. Can be placed on Arranger Timeline or Clip Launcher.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Clip Launcher', content: '**Clip Launcher** [performance] Nonlinear sequencer for accumulating musical ideas and mixing and matching them freely. Clips are organized into scenes for group triggering. Wann: Live performance, improvisation, loop-based composition, A/B testing different musical ideas before committing to arrangement.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Clip Launcher Panel', content: '**Clip Launcher Panel** [performance] Allows you to trigger clips both freely and in sync with the transport, copy clips into and out of the Arranger, and sort clips into scenes. Wann: When triggering and organizing clips for live performance or sequencing.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Container', content: '**Container** [device_category] Host other devices inside them — Instrument Layer, Instrument Selector, Multiband FX-2. Wann: Layering multiple instruments, round-robin switching, multiband processing.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Container Devices', content: '**Container Devices** [device] Utility devices designed to host other devices, enabling parallel device chains and complex routing. Wann: To organize and route signals to multiple devices simultaneously, such as layering instruments or effects.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Control Thru', content: '**Control Thru** [modulation] A parameter that allows Grid devices to pass through received non-note MIDI messages to the output. Wann: To maintain control signals when adding processing or modulation to a patch.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Creator filter', content: '**Creator filter** [browser] A filter that allows users to limit search results to those created by a specific person. Wann: Use when searching for content created by a specific individual or team.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Cue Markers', content: '**Cue Markers** [navigation] Cue markers store play positions along the Arranger Timeline for easy triggering. They allow for quick playback from specific points in the timeline. Wann: Use cue markers to quickly jump to specific sections of your arrangement for playback or editing.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Curve Editor', content: '**Curve Editor** [modulation] A resizable window that allows users to edit curves by selecting and adjusting points, drawing shapes, and modifying curvature. Wann: Used for creating and editing modulation curves in devices and modulators.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Deactivated Tracks button', content: '**Deactivated Tracks button** [navigation] Toggles the visibility of deactivated tracks within the Arranger Timeline Panel. Wann: For viewing or hiding tracks that are currently not active.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Detail Editor Panel', content: '**Detail Editor Panel** [editing] The graphical editor for both notes and audio, and their affiliated data. Wann: When making detailed edits to notes and audio events.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Detect Event Tempo', content: '**Detect Event Tempo** [automation] Runs tempo- and beat-detection analysis on selected audio clips with additional parameters like Tempo Range and Algorithm. Wann: To automatically detect the tempo and beat structure of an audio clip for synchronization.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Device', content: '**Device** [device] A special-function component that modifies or transforms incoming notes or audio signals. Each track has a device chain. Wann: To shape and process audio or MIDI signals within a track.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Device Chain', content: '**Device Chain** [device] The sequence of devices on a track. Signal flows left to right through the chain like a bucket brigade — each device processes the signal and passes it to the next. The final device returns audio to the track mixer. Wann: Building instrument+FX signal paths: synth → filter → distortion → reverb. Order matters — EQ before compression sounds different than after.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Device Nesting', content: '**Device Nesting** [device] A way to organize and apply devices to specific parts of a track or instrument, such as within a Drum Machine or Instrument Layer. Wann: To apply effects or instruments to specific notes or groups of notes within a track.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Device Panel', content: '**Device Panel** [device] The main panel showing the full device chain for the selected track. Signal always flows left (input) to right (output). Contains device headers with Enable button, Device Name, Remote Controls button, and Modulators button. Wann: Loading, editing, and routing devices. The central workspace for sound design.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Device filter', content: '**Device filter** [browser] A filter that allows users to limit search results to presets made with specific devices or categories of devices. Wann: Use when searching for presets compatible with specific devices or device types.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Devices Section', content: '**Devices Section** [device] Displays a list of all the top-level devices on each track, allowing for interaction with devices such as calling up the Device Panel, moving, copying, or adding new devices. Wann: Use to manage and manipulate devices on each track, such as editing parameters or adding new effects.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Display Section', content: '**Display Section** [navigation] Provides informational meters, numeric controls, and automation-related settings. Wann: For monitoring CPU usage, disk activity, tempo, time signature, and other playback metrics.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Drum Machine', content: '**Drum Machine** [device] A device designed to house multiple instruments, each triggered by a specific MIDI note message. Wann: When creating drum patterns with multiple drum sounds or percussion elements.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Dynamic', content: '**Dynamic** [device_category] Process audio based on amplitude levels — Compressor, Gate, Peak Limiter, Transient Control. Wann: Controlling dynamics: compressing peaks, gating noise, shaping transients.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:E-Tom', content: '**E-Tom** [device] An electronic tom instrument with optional pitch modulation. Wann: For creating electronic tom sounds with pitch modulation capabilities.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Edit View', content: '**Edit View** [navigation] For detail edits to clips. Detail Editor Panel is central with optional Automation Editor Panel. Wann: Editing individual MIDI notes, audio events, automation curves with full precision.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Envelope', content: '**Envelope** [modulation] A module that generates a time-based amplitude or control signal, typically with stages like Attack, Decay, Sustain, and Release. Wann: To shape the amplitude or modulation of a sound over time, such as for dynamic control of filters or effects.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Eraser tool', content: '**Eraser tool** [navigation] Used for deleting events from the selected area of time. Wann: For removing unwanted events from the timeline.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Expanded Device View', content: '**Expanded Device View** [device] Optional larger view available for specific devices (FM-4, Phase-4, Polysynth, Sampler, Compressor+, EQ+, EQ-5, Resonator Bank, Oscilloscope, Spectrum, Poly Grid, FX Grid, Note Grid, Polymer, Filter+, Sweep). Shows additional controls and visualizations. Wann: Detailed editing of complex devices. EQ-5 Expanded View shows the full EQ curve. Can be undocked as float'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:FX Grid', content: '**FX Grid** [device] A modular audio effect device with options for Voice Stacking and polyphonic effects. Wann: For designing unique audio effects with voice management capabilities.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:FX Layer', content: '**FX Layer** [device] A container that houses parallel audio chains, each with its own internal mixer controls. Wann: To apply multiple parallel effects to the same audio signal with independent control over each chain.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:FX Selector', content: '**FX Selector** [device] A container that houses multiple audio chains, allowing only one chain to receive incoming audio at a time. Wann: To switch between different audio processing chains based on triggers or automation.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:FX Tracks button', content: '**FX Tracks button** [navigation] Toggles the visibility of FX tracks within the Arranger Timeline Panel. Wann: For showing or hiding FX tracks in the timeline.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:File Kind Filter', content: '**File Kind Filter** [browser] A filter that isolates results to specific types of files, such as audio, MIDI, or device presets. Wann: To narrow down search results to a specific file type, such as only audio samples or device presets.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:File Kind filter', content: '**File Kind filter** [browser] A filter that allows users to select specific types of files, such as samples, presets, or impulses. Wann: Use to narrow down searches based on the type of file being looked for.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:File Type filter', content: '**File Type filter** [browser] A filter that appears when multiple file formats are selected, allowing users to narrow down results by file type. Wann: Use when searching for files with specific formats, such as audio or MIDI files.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Filter', content: '**Filter** [device_category] Frequency-specific processors — Filter+ (10 filter modules + 14 waveshapers), Sweep, Resonator Bank, Vocoder. Wann: Shaping timbre by cutting or boosting specific frequency ranges.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Filter Envelope Generator (FEG)', content: '**Filter Envelope Generator (FEG)** [modulation] A modulation source that controls the filter cutoff frequency over time, with Attack, Decay, Sustain, and Release controls. Wann: To shape the filter\'s response dynamically, adding movement and expression to the sound.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Filters Area', content: '**Filters Area** [browser] A section in the Browser that allows users to narrow down search results by applying filters such as location, file type, and more. Wann: To refine search results and focus on specific types of content or locations within the Bitwig library.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Fold to Takes', content: '**Fold to Takes** [arrangement] Takes any audio clip and wraps its material into successive take lanes, allowing for editing of individual takes. Wann: To break an audio clip into multiple takes for detailed editing or performance.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Follow Playback button', content: '**Follow Playback button** [navigation] Toggles whether to keep the Global Playhead on screen at all times in the Arranger Timeline Panel. Wann: For controlling the visibility of the playhead as you work.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Frequency Offset Control', content: '**Frequency Offset Control** [modulation] A numeric control that allows detuning of each oscillator by a specific number of Hertz. Wann: To fine-tune the pitch of an oscillator independently of its base frequency setting.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Gate In', content: '**Gate In** [device] Supplies note gate signals from the device. Wann: Use to trigger notes or events based on gate signals within a Grid device.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Global Playhead', content: '**Global Playhead** [navigation] An indicator showing where the transport has most recently played in the Arranger Timeline. Wann: To track the current playback position in the Arranger Timeline.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Grid Editor', content: '**Grid Editor** [device] An alternative view to the list editor that provides a visual grid layout for editing zones. Wann: Useful for quickly adjusting zones and their ranges with a visual layout.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Grid Modules', content: '**Grid Modules** [device] Building blocks that can be loaded within any Grid device and interconnected with other modules. Wann: Use to create custom modulation and signal routing setups within a Grid device.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Groove', content: '**Groove** [automation] Allows activation of shuffle for all clips with enabled Shuffle parameters. Includes Shuffle amount, Shuffle Rate, Accent amount, Accent Rate, and Accent Phase. Wann: For adding rhythmic variation and groove to clips.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Group Track', content: '**Group Track** [arrangement] A group track combines multiple tracks into a single track for streamlined mixing and editing. It has a folder icon that toggles visibility of its component tracks. Wann: To simplify mixing and editing multiple tracks as a single unit.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Hardware', content: '**Hardware** [navigation] Interface objects for sending signals and/or messages to devices beyond Bitwig Studio (such as hardware synthesizers and effect units). This can include transmitting and/or receiving audio signals, control voltage (CV) signals, and clock messages. Wann: When connecting external hardware synthesizers or effect units to Bitwig Studio.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:I/O Category', content: '**I/O Category** [device] Terminal modules for signals entering or exiting this Grid device. Wann: Use to manage input and output signals within a Grid device.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Inspector Panel', content: '**Inspector Panel** [navigation] Displays all parameters for any selected device, note, audio event, or track. Also shows modulation parameters for selected devices. Wann: Editing device parameters, viewing and adjusting note properties, setting track input/output.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Instrument Layer', content: '**Instrument Layer** [device] A container that houses multiple instruments in parallel, each with its own internal mixer controls. Wann: To layer multiple instruments or sounds simultaneously with independent control over each.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Instrument Selector', content: '**Instrument Selector** [device] A container that houses multiple instruments and their associated device chains, allowing only one instrument chain to receive new notes at a time. Wann: To switch between different instrument layers based on note input, MIDI controllers, or automation.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Intensity Offset', content: '**Intensity Offset** [device] Adjusts the compression curve for a specific frequency band, with positive values increasing compression and negative values reducing it. Wann: To fine-tune the compression behavior of individual frequency bands.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Interface Category', content: '**Interface Category** [navigation] Provides panel elements for better/unique control or access to Transport-level interface items. Wann: For creating custom UI controls or integrating with transport functions in projects.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Joint Frequency Control', content: '**Joint Frequency Control** [device] A control that simultaneously adjusts the frequency of two filters in a device, with a range of ±3 octaves. Wann: To control both filters in a device with a single parameter for dynamic frequency modulation.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Knife tool', content: '**Knife tool** [navigation] Used for splitting a continuous event into two. Wann: For dividing events at specific points in time.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:LFO (Low Frequency Oscillator)', content: '**LFO (Low Frequency Oscillator)** [modulation] A periodic oscillator that generates low-frequency signals for modulation purposes, often used to create movement in sound. Wann: To modulate parameters like filter cutoff, pan, or volume over time for dynamic effects.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Launcher Clips', content: '**Launcher Clips** [performance] Clips that can be triggered at any time, offering flexibility for live performance or improvisation. Wann: For live performance, section-based composition, or spontaneous musical ideas.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Launcher Slot', content: '**Launcher Slot** [performance] An individual slot within a scene that can hold a clip. Slots can be used for recording or triggering clips. Wann: To record or trigger individual clips within a scene.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:List Editor', content: '**List Editor** [device] A view that shows only the zones currently on-screen and allows for detailed editing of each zone\'s parameters. Wann: Ideal for managing and editing zones in a multisample when working with a large number of samples.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Location Filter', content: '**Location Filter** [browser] A filter that lets users specify the source of content, such as local libraries, Bitwig Studio content, or installed packages. Wann: To filter content based on its origin, such as searching only within the user\'s local library or installed packages.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:MIDI', content: '**MIDI** [navigation] Transmitters for sending various MIDI messages via the track\'s device chain. Wann: When sending messages to plug-ins or external hardware (when used in conjunction with Bitwig\'s hardware devices).'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Mappings Browser Panel', content: '**Mappings Browser Panel** [automation] Allows you to make and edit project-specific connections of your computer keyboard and/or MIDI controller(s) to your project\'s parameters. Wann: When mapping keyboard or MIDI controller inputs to project parameters.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Master Track', content: '**Master Track** [mixing] The master track is the final output track in a project. It sums all signals and provides access to transport parameters like tempo. Wann: To control and monitor the final mix of a project.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Mid-Side Split', content: '**Mid-Side Split** [device] A container that splits a stereo signal into mid (centered) and side (panned) components, each with an independent chain. Wann: To apply different processing to the center and stereo components of a stereo signal.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Mix Parameter', content: '**Mix Parameter** [modulation] A parameter found on many Bitwig devices that controls the blend between dry (original) and wet (processed) signals. Wann: To blend original and processed signals in effects like delay or frequency shifting.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Mix View', content: '**Mix View** [mixing] Focuses on mixing tracks and triggering clips. Mixer Panel is central, with optional Clip Launcher Panel. All panels except Arranger Timeline are available. Wann: Balancing levels, setting sends/returns, EQ and compression, live clip triggering during mixing.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Mixer Panel', content: '**Mixer Panel** [mixing] Presents the channel strip for each track and any subsidiary signal chains. Central to the Mix View. Wann: Adjusting levels, panning, sends, and FX routing across all tracks simultaneously.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulation', content: '**Modulation** [device_category] Manipulate audio with LFOs etc. — Chorus+, Flanger, Phaser+, Rotary, Tremolo. Wann: Adding movement and animation to sounds — chorus width, tremolo rhythm, flanger sweeps.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulation Enable/Disable Toggle', content: '**Modulation Enable/Disable Toggle** [modulation] A button that enables or disables an oscillator or noise generator for modulation purposes. Wann: To bypass modulation contributions from a specific oscillator or noise generator.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulation Matrix', content: '**Modulation Matrix** [modulation] A table showing the individual amounts of modulation between the five generators (four oscillators and one noise generator). Columns represent modulation sources, and rows represent modulation destinations. Wann: To create complex frequency modulation relationships between oscillators and the noise generator.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulation Routing', content: '**Modulation Routing** [modulation] A method to connect a modulation source to multiple destinations with individual modulation amounts. The modulation routing button resembles an output port with a patch cord coming out of it. Wann: To apply modulation effects to multiple parameters simultaneously, such as modulating filter cutoff and resonance with an LFO.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulation Routings', content: '**Modulation Routings** [modulation] Modulation routings define how a modulation source affects a target parameter. These can be manipulated, duplicated, or cleared. Wann: To create dynamic and expressive modulation effects within a device or across multiple modulators.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulation Sources', content: '**Modulation Sources** [modulation] Modulation sources generate control signals that can be applied to various parameters. They can be either monophonic (one signal for all targets) or polyphonic (unique signal for each note). Wann: To shape sound dynamically, such as modulating filters, oscillators, or effects in real-time.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulation Sources Tab', content: '**Modulation Sources Tab** [modulation] The first tab in the Inspector Panel that lists modulation sources for the selected device, along with modulation routing buttons. Wann: Used to manage and route modulation sources for dynamic control of parameters.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulator', content: '**Modulator** [modulation] Special-purpose modules loaded into any device (or onto any track) that animate parameters over time. Can control any device parameter in the chain. Wann: LFO on filter cutoff for movement, envelope follower for ducking, MIDI expression for dynamic control.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulator Devices', content: '**Modulator Devices** [modulation] Devices that allow for modulation assignments within Bitwig Studio, enabling dynamic control over parameters. Wann: To dynamically control parameters such as pitch, filter cutoff, or volume using modulation sources like LFOs or envelopes.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulators', content: '**Modulators** [modulation] Special-purpose modules that are made to be loaded into any device to allow a particular method of control over device parameters. Wann: To add control over device parameters using various modulation sources and methods.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Modulators Pane', content: '**Modulators Pane** [modulation] A pane that allows users to load and manage modulator devices for modulation purposes. Wann: To add, manage, and toggle modulator devices for modulation control.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Module Palette', content: '**Module Palette** [navigation] A browser for Grid modules, allowing users to search, filter, and preview modules by category. Wann: To find and select modules for inclusion in the Grid.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Multiband FX-2', content: '**Multiband FX-2** [device] A container that splits incoming audio at a definable frequency, providing independent chains for the audio below and above that frequency. Wann: To apply different effects to different frequency ranges of an audio signal.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Multiband FX-3', content: '**Multiband FX-3** [device] A container that splits incoming audio at two definable frequencies, providing independent chains for the audio below the first frequency, between the two frequencies, and above the second frequency. Wann: To apply different effects to three distinct frequency ranges of an audio signal.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Multisample Editor', content: '**Multisample Editor** [device] A view that displays detailed information about each zone, including key range, velocity range, and select parameter range, with visualizations of crossfading. Wann: Used to precisely edit and manage multisample zones for detailed control over playback ranges and transitions.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Nested Device Chains', content: '**Nested Device Chains** [device] A structure where devices are organized in chains for processing audio signals, with specific I/O configurations for each container type. Wann: To process audio signals with multiple devices in a structured and flexible way.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Note FX', content: '**Note FX** [device_category] Manipulate note signals before passing onward — Arpeggiator, Multi-note, Note Repeats. Wann: Generating arpeggios, chord stacks, rhythmic note patterns from held notes.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Note FX Layer', content: '**Note FX Layer** [device] A container that houses parallel note chains. Wann: When you want to process multiple note chains simultaneously.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Note FX Selector', content: '**Note FX Selector** [device] A container that houses multiple note chains. Only one note chain at a time receives the incoming notes, but any chain that was previously outputting notes remains active until its output has ceased. Wann: When you want to switch between different note chains while maintaining the output of previously active chains until they finish.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Note Grid', content: '**Note Grid** [device] A modular note processor or generator with options for Voice Stacking and polyphonic effects. Wann: For creating complex note processing or generation with voice management.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Note Thru', content: '**Note Thru** [modulation] A parameter that allows Grid devices to pass through received note signals to the output. Wann: To maintain note signals when adding processing or modulation to a patch.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Object Selection', content: '**Object Selection** [navigation] Starts with choosing one or more timeline objects (such as clips, audio events, note events, expression points, or automation points). This is usually achieved by clicking objects with the Pointer tool. Wann: When selecting specific timeline objects for editing or manipulation.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:On-Screen Keyboard Panel', content: '**On-Screen Keyboard Panel** [performance] A panel at the bottom of the Play View for note entry and monitoring. Wann: Used for playing and creating notes on a tablet.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:On-screen Keyboard Panel', content: '**On-screen Keyboard Panel** [performance] Provides visualizations of the selected track\'s playing and incoming note messages, pitch expressions, and timbre expressions, as well as an input method for these data streams. Wann: When visualizing and inputting note messages, pitch expressions, and timbre expressions.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Oscillator Modulation Control', content: '**Oscillator Modulation Control** [modulation] A control that attenuates the output of the oscillator to all frequency modulation connections without affecting the audio output. Wann: To control how much an oscillator contributes to frequency modulation without altering its audio output.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Output Monitoring Panel', content: '**Output Monitoring Panel** [mixing] Gives audio control options, such as routing the main audio buss to any pairs of speakers and headphones, solo and cue behaviors, etc. Wann: When monitoring and routing audio output to different devices.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Output Paths', content: '**Output Paths** [mixing] Configurable audio output paths that can be assigned to specific roles like Speakers or Output. Wann: To direct audio to different speakers or external hardware.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Parameter', content: '**Parameter** [device] A setting that determines how a device operates. Parameters can be adjusted via MIDI, automation, or modulators. Wann: To fine-tune the behavior of a device or effect.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Pen tool', content: '**Pen tool** [navigation] Used for drawing new events. Wann: For creating new events directly in the timeline.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Per-Voice Mode', content: '**Per-Voice Mode** [modulation] A toggle that allows modulators to switch between monophonic and polyphonic modes, enabling unique modulation per note. Wann: When creating complex, evolving sounds that require individual modulation per note, such as polyphonic modulation for synths.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Phase Distortion', content: '**Phase Distortion** [modulation] A method of waveform shaping where the path of the waveshape is altered as the SHAPE parameter is increased, determined by the selected algorithm. Wann: To create unique timbres and evolving sounds by manipulating the waveform\'s shape and phase.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Phase Modulation', content: '**Phase Modulation** [modulation] A technique that modulates the phase of the waveform, controlled by the MOD knob and individual oscillator modulation levels. Wann: To add movement and complexity to the waveform by altering its phase dynamically.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Phase-4', content: '**Phase-4** [device] A phase-manipulation synthesizer with four unique oscillator units, global phase controls, and a multimode filter. Wann: To create complex sounds using phase distortion and phase modulation techniques.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Play Start Marker', content: '**Play Start Marker** [navigation] A blue, right-facing triangle in the Beat Ruler that indicates where the transport will play from the next time it is engaged. Wann: To set the starting point for playback in the Arranger Timeline.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Play View', content: '**Play View** [performance] A view available only in the Tablet display profile, primarily for note entry via the tablet\'s touch screen. Wann: Used for note entry on a tablet computer.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Playback', content: '**Playback** [navigation] Provides parameters that take effect during project playback, such as Metronome volume, Play Ticks, and Fill mode toggle. Wann: For configuring playback settings and metronome behavior.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Plug-in Hosting Mode', content: '**Plug-in Hosting Mode** [device] A setting that determines how isolated each plug-in process is within Bitwig Studio. It affects resource usage and crash safety. Wann: Use to manage plug-in stability and resource allocation based on your system\'s capabilities and workflow needs.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Pointer tool', content: '**Pointer tool** [navigation] Used for selecting and moving objects such as clips, audio and note events, or automation points. Wann: For precise selection and manipulation of events in the timeline.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Poly Grid', content: '**Poly Grid** [device] A device for creating instruments, including mono/poly-synths, samplers, sequenced patches, and drones. Wann: For building versatile instruments with polyphonic capabilities.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Polyphonic Modulation', content: '**Polyphonic Modulation** [modulation] Modulation sources that operate independently for each note played, allowing for unique modulation behavior per voice. Wann: Useful for creating rich, evolving textures where each note has its own modulation characteristics.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Pop-out Editor', content: '**Pop-out Editor** [device] A resizeable interface for custom modules, offering editing functions like nudge backward and forward buttons, especially useful in The Grid for distinguishing between patching mode and data editing mode. Wann: Used for detailed editing of modules like the Pitches (Data) sequencer in The Grid.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Pop-out Editors', content: '**Pop-out Editors** [modulation] Some modulators and modules have a resizeable Pop-out Editor for editing playback data, such as curves. Wann: To edit and manipulate modulation data in detail, similar to how automation or audio is edited.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Pre-cords', content: '**Pre-cords** [device] Pre-configured wireless connections in Grid patches that simplify common module connections. Wann: To avoid clutter and streamline patch creation by using pre-configured connections.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Project', content: '**Project** [navigation] A file created in Bitwig Studio. Multiple projects can be open at once, but only one will have active audio. Wann: To organize and work on different music compositions or sessions.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Project Panel', content: '**Project Panel** [navigation] Manages your project\'s metadata, gives access to all Arranger cue markers and Launcher scenes, and shows the status of files and plug-ins being used. Wann: When managing project metadata and tracking file and plug-in status.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Quantize', content: '**Quantize** [automation] Shifts the start/end times of discrete audio events toward the beat grid. It uses the most recently set parameters for the function. Wann: To align audio events with the beat grid for rhythmic precision.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Quantize Audio', content: '**Quantize Audio** [automation] A high-level variation on the Quantize function that creates beat markers from certain onsets and shifts them closer to the beat grid, effectively quantizing the audio internally. Wann: To precisely align audio content with the beat grid by analyzing internal onsets.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Replacer', content: '**Replacer** [device] A container that filters and analyzes the level of the incoming audio signal, and when the signal rises above a set threshold, notes are generated at a set pitch and velocity. These notes and the original (dry) audio signal are then passed to the internal Generator device chain. Wann: When you want to generate MIDI notes based on audio input levels.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Reverb', content: '**Reverb** [fx] Time-based processors that operate on their incoming audio signals. Wann: When adding spatial depth and ambiance to audio signals.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Routing', content: '**Routing** [device_category] Divert track signal paths — Audio Receiver (bring audio from another track), Note Receiver. Wann: Sidechain routing, parallel processing, sending signal to multiple destinations.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Routing Blend', content: '**Routing Blend** [device] A control that smoothly transitions between different signal routing configurations within a device. Wann: To blend between parallel and serial signal routing configurations for complex sound design.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Sampler', content: '**Sampler** [device] A device that can handle single or multiple samples in zones with resizable mapping editors, multiple play modes, a multimode filter, and numerous modulation opportunities. Wann: When you need to play back audio files as source material with flexible playback and modulation options.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Scene', content: '**Scene** [performance] A group of Launcher clips across multiple tracks that can be triggered together simultaneously. Wann: Triggering full sections (verse, chorus, drop) at once during live performance or composition.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Seed Section', content: '**Seed Section** [automation] A feature that controls randomized parameters in clips, including expression Spread values and Chance Operators. Wann: To generate and reproduce consistent randomized patterns in clips.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Segments', content: '**Segments** [modulation] A freely drawable, segmented envelope generator with BWCURVE file support, using the Curve Editor. It allows for custom envelope shapes with multiple points and various play modes. Wann: For creating complex, customizable envelope shapes for modulation or sound design.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Send / Return', content: '**Send / Return** [mixing] FX tracks receive audio via send amounts from instrument/audio tracks. Each track has a send level controlling how much signal goes to each FX track. FX tracks show an Effect Sends Pane listing all source tracks. Wann: Shared reverb or delay for multiple tracks. Set FX track device to 100% wet, adjust per-track send levels to taste.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Send Section', content: '**Send Section** [mixing] A section in the mixer interface that allows routing audio from a track to an FX track using send knobs. Wann: To route audio to FX tracks without affecting the main output level of the source track.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Settings', content: '**Settings** [navigation] Offers workflow parameters including Pre-roll controls, Record Quantization settings, and other playback-related options. Wann: For configuring project settings and playback behavior.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Sidechain FX Slot', content: '**Sidechain FX Slot** [device] A slot for pre-processing the analysis input, allowing for sidechain configurations. Wann: To apply effects or processing to the analysis input before compression.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Slice In Place', content: '**Slice In Place** [arrangement] Divides the selected clip into multiple clips, slicing regularly at a note interval (on Beat Grid), or at Onsets or Beat Markers. Wann: To efficiently edit audio clips by dividing them into smaller, manageable segments.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Slice to Drum Machine', content: '**Slice to Drum Machine** [arrangement] Produces a new instrument track loaded with a Drum Machine device, containing audio clips representing the original clip\'s content. Wann: To convert an audio clip into a drum pattern using a Drum Machine.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Slice to Multisample', content: '**Slice to Multisample** [arrangement] Produces a new instrument track loaded with a Sampler device, whose multiple samples represent the original clip\'s content. Wann: To convert an audio clip into a multisampled instrument for playback.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Smart Collections', content: '**Smart Collections** [browser] A dynamic, saved set of filters that creates a real entity in the Bitwig browser, allowing it to be used as a source for other operations. Wann: To create a reusable, dynamic collection of content based on specific filter criteria.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Spectral', content: '**Spectral** [device_category] Operate in frequency domain — Transient Split, Loud Split, Harmonic Split. Wann: Separating percussive from tonal content, splitting harmonics for parallel processing.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Spectral Limiter', content: '**Spectral Limiter** [device] A feature that caps individual frequency bands at a set threshold to prevent certain frequency ranges from exceeding a specified level. Wann: Use to control dynamic range in specific frequency bands without affecting the overall mix.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Spectrum Display', content: '**Spectrum Display** [device] A visual representation of the frequency spectrum, showing either pre-processing or post-processing audio data. Wann: Use to analyze and monitor frequency content before or after processing.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Stack Spread', content: '**Stack Spread** [device] A modulation mode that offers 12 spread modes for varying all voices in a stack with one modulation mapping to any parameter(s). Wann: Use to create unique modulation effects across multiple voices in a stack.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Stack Spread Modulator', content: '**Stack Spread Modulator** [modulation] Offers 12 spread modes to control how modulation is distributed across voices in a stack. Wann: Useful for creating varied modulation effects across multiple voices in a stack.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Stereo Independence', content: '**Stereo Independence** [device] Controls the amount of separation between the stereo channels of each analysis band. Wann: To adjust stereo imaging for each frequency band independently.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Stereo Pan', content: '**Stereo Pan** [device] A control that adjusts the stereo positioning of filters in a device, affecting left and right channel cutoffs. Wann: To create stereo width and spatial movement in filter cutoffs.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Stereo Split', content: '**Stereo Split** [device] A container that takes a normal stereo signal and splits it into its left and right channels, each of which is provided with an independent chain. Wann: When you want to process the left and right channels of a stereo signal independently.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Synth', content: '**Synth** [instrument] Synthesizer instruments that either generate their audio from rudimentary source material or use audio samples. Incoming note messages are used to synthesize audio. Wann: When creating synthesized sounds or using audio samples as instruments.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Tags', content: '**Tags** [browser] A special filter in the browser that helps narrow searches using assigned keywords. It uses four columns to display more information at once, with the top 16 slots showing consistent tags from Bitwig\'s content and the lower section displaying relevant tags sorted alphabetically. Wann: To quickly filter and find specific content in the browser by using keywords or tags associated'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:The Grid', content: '**The Grid** [device] Bitwig\'s modular sound-design environment. Accessed through Poly Grid (instrument), FX Grid (audio effect), and Note Grid (note processor). Build custom signal paths from modular components. Wann: Creating custom synthesizers, generative sequencers, complex modulation sources, or unique FX chains not possible with standard devices.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Time Selection', content: '**Time Selection** [navigation] Captures any events (or partial events) within a span of time. This is usually achieved with the Time Selection tool. Wann: When selecting events within a specific time range for editing or manipulation.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Time Selection tool', content: '**Time Selection tool** [navigation] Used for selecting an arbitrary section of time instead of specific events. Wann: For making time-based selections in the timeline.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Time Signature Changes', content: '**Time Signature Changes** [arrangement] Time signature changes can be inserted along the Arranger Timeline to define different rhythmic structures in your arrangement. Wann: Use time signature changes to create complex rhythmic patterns or transitions in your composition.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Timing Offset', content: '**Timing Offset** [device] Adjusts the timing parameters for a specific frequency band, with positive values increasing the timing and negative values decreasing it. Wann: To control the timing response of compression for individual frequency bands.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Tom', content: '**Tom** [device] A drum element instrument that synthesizes audio using incoming note signals. Wann: For creating tom drum sounds with synthesized audio.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track', content: '**Track** [mixing] Individual instrument or audio layer in a project. Each track has a device chain and standard mixing controls (volume, pan, solo, mute). Signal flows through device chain before reaching mixer. Wann: Organizing instruments, audio recordings, FX returns, and groups in a project.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track Colors and Color Palettes', content: '**Track Colors and Color Palettes** [navigation] Each track is assigned a color when created, and this color can be changed. Users can select from predefined palettes or add custom color palettes by dragging image files into the Bitwig window. Wann: To visually organize and distinguish tracks in the Arrange view for easier navigation and workflow efficiency.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track Deactivation', content: '**Track Deactivation** [performance] Tracks can be deactivated to silence their output and reduce CPU load temporarily. This is useful for managing resources without losing data. Wann: To temporarily disable tracks that are not in use to optimize CPU performance.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track Header', content: '**Track Header** [navigation] The track header is used to select and manage tracks. It changes color and appearance based on selection status. Wann: To select, group, and manage tracks efficiently.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track Headers', content: '**Track Headers** [navigation] The section to the left of the Arrange area that contains track-specific controls and information. Wann: Used to manage track settings, such as color, type, name, volume, and mute/solo states.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track Height button', content: '**Track Height button** [navigation] Toggles the track height in the Arranger between normal and half size. Wann: For adjusting the visual size of track headers in the Arranger.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track I/O Settings', content: '**Track I/O Settings** [navigation] A section in each track header that allows assigning input and output paths for tracks. Wann: To route MIDI or audio signals correctly into and out of a track.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track I/O button', content: '**Track I/O button** [navigation] Toggles the visibility of the Track I/O section of all track headers. Wann: For viewing or hiding track input/output settings.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track Remotes Section', content: '**Track Remotes Section** [mixing] Provides remote controls for each track directly on the Mixer Panel, allowing parameter controls from different tracks to be visualized and interacted with side by side. Wann: Use to have direct access and control over track parameters for easier mixing and automation.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Track Types', content: '**Track Types** [arrangement] Tracks in Bitwig Studio are categorized into instrument, audio, hybrid, FX, group, and master tracks, each with specific icons and purposes. Wann: To organize and manage different types of audio and MIDI content within a project.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Transient Split', content: '**Transient Split** [device] A device that separates transients (short, unstable sounds) and tones (periodic or pitched sounds) for individual mixing and processing. Wann: For isolating and processing transients and tones separately in a mix.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Transport', content: '**Transport** [navigation] The global play, stop, and record engine that drives all time functions in Bitwig. Any clip must have an active transport to play back or be triggered. Wann: Starting/stopping playback, setting BPM, enabling loop recording, controlling global playhead position.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Transpose', content: '**Transpose** [modulation] Adjusts the pitch of note events or the pitch expression of audio events by a specified interval. Wann: To shift the pitch of audio or MIDI clips up or down for tuning or creative purposes.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Unified Modulation System', content: '**Unified Modulation System** [modulation] A program-wide method in Bitwig Studio for assigning and editing modulations, allowing for flexible modulation routings and preserving parameter control. Wann: To easily assign and edit modulations without being restricted by fixed modulation routings, and to maintain control over modulated parameters.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Utility', content: '**Utility** [fx] An assortment of devices sporting various generating, processing, and time-shifting functionality. Wann: When applying general-purpose audio processing or time-shifting effects.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:VCA Color Mode', content: '**VCA Color Mode** [device] A feature that determines the tonal character applied to the compressed signal, only affecting portions of the signal that experience gain change. Wann: To add tonal character or warmth to the compressed signal, depending on the selected mode.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Vendor filter', content: '**Vendor filter** [browser] A filter that appears for plug-ins and device-based selections, allowing users to narrow results by manufacturer. Wann: Use when searching for specific plug-in manufacturers or device creators.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Voice Control', content: '**Voice Control** [device] Provides individual control of each voice with an active voice stack, with individual modulators for Stack Voice 1 through Stack Voice 16 directly in the modulator square. Wann: Use to fine-tune each voice in a stack independently for more detailed modulation and control.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Voice Control Modulator', content: '**Voice Control Modulator** [modulation] Provides individual control over each voice in a voice stack. Wann: Useful for fine-tuning each voice in a stack for unique modulation effects.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Voice Parameters', content: '**Voice Parameters** [device] Voice-related parameters in Bitwig Studio instruments and Grid-powered devices that control polyphony and monophonic behavior. Wann: Used to control the number of active voices and how they behave in polyphonic or monophonic modes.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Voice Solo', content: '**Voice Solo** [performance] A feature that allows soloing individual voices within a voice stack. It can be disabled to hear all voices simultaneously. Wann: Use when you want to disable solo mode in a voice stack to hear all voices at once.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Voice Stacking', content: '**Voice Stacking** [device] A feature that allows devices to work differently on each individual voice within an active voice stack. Wann: Use when you want to create complex, layered sounds with variations across multiple voices.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:Wet/Dry Mix', content: '**Wet/Dry Mix** [device] Controls the amount of processed signal at the device output, blended with the dry audio signal. Wann: To create parallel compression effects by blending the compressed and original signals.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:XY FX', content: '**XY FX** [device] A container that loads up to four audio effects in parallel and allows you to crossfade their outputs. Wann: When you want to blend multiple audio effects in real-time using a crossfade.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:XY Instrument', content: '**XY Instrument** [device] A container that loads up to four instruments in parallel and allows you to crossfade their outputs. Wann: When you want to blend multiple instruments in real-time using a crossfade.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Concept:X–Y Pad', content: '**X–Y Pad** [modulation] A control surface that allows simultaneous adjustment of the global SHAPE and MOD parameters for all oscillators. Wann: To quickly and intuitively shape the overall phase distortion and modulation of multiple oscillators at once.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:4-Stage', content: '**4-Stage** A looping four-stage envelope generator with definable times and levels. Verwendung: To create rhythmic or dynamic modulation envelopes. Parameter: Stage Level: Controls the level for each stage of the envelope. (niedrig=Low level, minimal modulation., hoch=High level, maximum modulation.). Tipp: Adjust Stage Level to control the intensity of each envelope stage.; Stage Time: Controls '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:=', content: '**=** Comparator assessing if two signals are roughly equal Verwendung: Used for comparing two signals Tipps: Use this device to compare two signals for equality'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:AD', content: '**AD** A two-stage triggered envelope generator with amplifier, looping mode, and three Model options. Verwendung: For creating simple envelope shapes with precise control over attack and decay stages. Kategorie: envelope Parameter: Model: Analog / Relative / Digital curve shape (niedrig=Analog curves, hoch=Digital linear); Loop: Looping mode — rereleases trigger on completion (niedrig=one-shot en'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:ADSR', content: '**ADSR** Four-stage gated envelope generator with amplifier Verwendung: Creating dynamic amplitude envelopes for sounds Kategorie: envelope Parameter: Model: Selects the envelope model (A, R, or D) for different envelope behaviors. (niedrig=Model A provides a standard ADSR envelope., hoch=Model D provides a different envelope behavior based on the selected option.). Tipp: Experiment with different'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:AHD on Release', content: '**AHD on Release** An attack–hold–decay envelope generator triggered by note off messages, with a Single Trigger option and individual curve controls for each segment. Verwendung: For creating release envelopes with precise control over attack, hold, and decay stages. Tipps: Use the Single Trigger option to prevent unwanted retriggering during overlapping notes.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:AHDSR', content: '**AHDSR** A standard envelope generator with attack, hold, decay, sustain, and release segments, with individual curve controls and options for Single Trigger and pre-delay. Verwendung: For dynamic envelope shaping in synthesis or modulation tasks. Tipps: Use the pre-delay feature to add space or delay before the envelope starts.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:AM/RM', content: '**AM/RM** Crossfades between dry carrier, classic amplitude modulation, and ring modulation Verwendung: Switching between modulation types'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:AND', content: '**AND** A logic gate that outputs true only if all inputs are true. Verwendung: Used for creating conditional processing that requires multiple conditions to be met. Tipps: Can be used to trigger events when multiple signals are active simultaneously.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:AR', content: '**AR** A three-stage gated envelope generator with amplifier and three Model options. Verwendung: For creating more complex envelope shapes with attack, release, and sustain stages. Kategorie: envelope Parameter: Model: Curve model: Analog / Relative / Digital (niedrig=Analog curves, hoch=Digital linear); Release: Fall time after gate closes (niedrig=abrupt cutoff, hoch=long release tail); Attack:'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Abs', content: '**Abs** Separates a signal into its magnitude and sign components Verwendung: Use to extract the absolute value of a signal for processing or analysis.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Add', content: '**Add** Adds two signals together Verwendung: Use to combine two signals for layering or mixing.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:All-pass', content: '**All-pass** All-pass filter with adjustable slope Verwendung: For creating phase shifts and resonant effects without altering the frequency content'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:All-pass Delay', content: '**All-pass Delay** All-pass filter configured as a delay Verwendung: Creating delay effects using an all-pass filter'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Amp', content: '**Amp** A processor that applies the character of various instrument amplifiers to the incoming signal. Includes PRE and POST drive stages with EQ, drive, bias, sag, and cabinet simulation. Verwendung: Simulating the tone of different guitar amplifiers with EQ shaping and cabinet modeling. Parameter: Global Mix: Controls the blend between the pre-cabinet and post-cabinet signals. (niedrig=More pre'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Amplify', content: '**Amplify** Signal amplifier set in percentage (up to 800 %) Verwendung: Boosting signal amplitude up to 800% Kategorie: utility Parameter: Gain: Amplification factor (niedrig=signal reduced to zero, hoch=signal amplified 8x). Tipp: Use Amplify before Wavefolder to drive it harder'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Amplitude Envelope Generator (AEG)', content: '**Amplitude Envelope Generator (AEG)** Controls the amplitude envelope of the instrument with Attack, Decay, Sustain, and Release parameters. Verwendung: Shaping the dynamic contour of the instrument\'s sound. Parameter: Release: Controls the time it takes for the envelope to decay to zero after the note is released. (niedrig=Immediate release, hoch=Longer release for a more gradual fade-out). Tipp'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Arpeggiator', content: '**Arpeggiator** An MPE-friendly arpeggiator that cycles through the notes being held in a set order. It has 17 note patterns and three different Octave Behavior modes. Verwendung: Creating rhythmic note patterns and sequences. Parameter: Randomize: Adds randomness to velocity, timing, and duration of notes. (niedrig=No randomness, hoch=Maximum randomness). Tipp: Use for more organic or unpredictab'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Array', content: '**Array** Recordable lookup table Verwendung: Used to store and recall custom lookup tables for phase signals'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Attenuate', content: '**Attenuate** Signal attenuator Verwendung: Reducing signal amplitude'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Audio Instrument', content: '**Audio Instrument** Plays back one or more audio files as source material. It includes waveform display and parameters for controlling playback, pitch, and looping. Verwendung: Use for sampling and manipulating audio files for playback, including pitch shifting and looping. Parameter: Zone Edges: Adjusts the edges of a sample zone by dragging the edges. (niedrig=Zone is narrower or shorter, hoch='});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Audio MOD', content: '**Audio MOD** A modulator that applies a filter and envelope follower to an incoming audio signal, which is then used as the control signal. Verwendung: For using audio signals as control signals. Tipps: Useful for creating dynamic modulation based on audio input.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Audio Out', content: '**Audio Out** A module that outputs audio and can be used to control voice activation based on audio levels. Verwendung: To keep voices active until the audio signal falls below a certain threshold. Tipps: Enable Affect Voice Lifetime to control voice sustain based on audio levels.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Device:Audio Rate', content: '**Audio Rate** An instantaneous sidechain control with gain control, optional low-pass filter, and a Rectify switch for converting signals to positive values. Verwendung: Used for dynamic sidechain control with real-time signal processing. Parameter: Rectify: Converts the incoming signal to all positive values. (niedrig=Signal remains as-is with negative and positive values., hoch=Signal is rectif'});
