MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:5-Reverb-Send-Architektur', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **5-Reverb-Send-Architektur** [Drum and Bass] Quelle: Ferrous Rhythm Statt eines einzigen Reverbs werden 5 spezialisierte Return-Tracks mit unterschiedlichen Reverb-Charakteren aufgebaut: Small Room (kurz, trocken), Gated Reverb (80er-Style), Falling Hall (melodisch abfallend), Long Plate (klassisch), Time Dilation (synthetisch, experimentell). Jedes Element im Mix bekommt gezi'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Acoustic Drum Multi-Mic Sampler Mapping', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Acoustic Drum Multi-Mic Sampler Mapping** [Acoustic] Quelle: play-drums Echte Schlagzeug-Aufnahmen werden mit mehreren Mikrofon-Positionen gesampelt: Kick (DW 1, DW Nofront), Snare (DW 13 1 C, Edge, Rimshot, Rim), Toms (DW Stick 1-4). Jede Mikrofon-Variante ist ein eigenes Sampler-Preset. Das Drum Machine Device kombiniert diese Presets zu einem vollständigen Kit. Wann: Reali'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Classic Drum Machine Pack Layering (606/707/808/909)', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Classic Drum Machine Pack Layering (606/707/808/909)** [Electronic] Quelle: play-drums Bitwig\'s Classic Drum Machines Pack enthält vier legendäre Drum Machines: Legend 606 (crispy elektronisch), Legend 707 (cleaner Funk-Sound), Legend 808 (subbasiger Boom, 808 Kick/Clap), Legend 909 (Acid/Techno, kurze snappige Kicks). Per Szene oder Mute/Solo lassen sich verschiedene Drum-Ch'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Convolution Reverb für Räumlichkeit', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Convolution Reverb für Räumlichkeit** [Drum and Bass] Quelle: Ferrous Rhythm Convolution-Reverb mit verschiedenen Impulse Responses (IRs) erzeugt realistische Räume die algorithmische Reverbs nicht replizieren können. Verschiedene IRs für verschiedene Räume (Halls, Plates, Springs, Synthetic). Convolution klingt in hochwertigem DnB, Electronica, Ambient natürlicher. Wann: Wen'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:DnB Reese Bass via Phase-4', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **DnB Reese Bass via Phase-4** [Drum and Bass] Quelle: Ferrous Rhythm Klassischer Reese-Bass mit dem Phase-4-Synthesizer durch Detuning zweier Sägezahn-Oszillatoren. Phase-4 erzeugt durch sein Phasenmodulations-System ein besonders organisches Beating zwischen den Oszillatoren. Wann: Klassischer DnB/Neurofunk Reese-Bass. Phase-4 klingt organischer als FM-4 für Reese-Sounds durc'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Drum Machine mit Bitwig-Pack-Layering', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Drum Machine mit Bitwig-Pack-Layering** [Drum and Bass] Quelle: Ferrous Rhythm Das Drum Machine Device nutzt mehrere Bitwig-Drum-Packs simultan: v0 (klassische elektronische Drums), v1 (Alternative-Sounds), v8 (Clap-Variationen), v9 (Ride/Cymbal). Mehrere Pack-Sounds werden auf demselben Pad gelayert um komplexere Percussion-Charaktere zu erzeugen. Wann: Professionelle DnB-Dr'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Drums Processor Mix Chain (Gruppen-FX mit Preset)', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Drums Processor Mix Chain (Gruppen-FX mit Preset)** [Electronic] Quelle: play-drums Das \'Drums Processor - Mix 1\' Preset ist ein Chain-Device mit einer vordefinierten Multi-FX-Kette für Drum-Gruppen: EQ-Korrekturen, Transient-Shaping, leichte Kompression, Sättigung. Als Gruppen-Insert statt auf Einzelspuren spart es CPU und sorgt für kohärenten Drum-Klang. Wann: Drum-Gruppe p'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:FM-4 für Keyboard-Sounds (Piano, Bell, Organ)', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **FM-4 für Keyboard-Sounds (Piano, Bell, Organ)** [Electronic] Quelle: performance-set Das Performance-Set zeigt FM-4 nicht nur für Bässe sondern für melodische Keyboard-Sounds: FM Piano 4 (klassischer FM-Piano-Sound wie DX7), Singer In Alps (atmosphärischer Gesangssound). FM-Synthese erzeugt charakteristische Anschlag-Transients die Sample-basierte E-Pianos kaum replizieren kö'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:FX Grid Custom Sidechain Patch', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **FX Grid Custom Sidechain Patch** [Drum and Bass] Quelle: Ferrous Rhythm Ein eigener Sidechain-Kompressor-Patch wurde im FX Grid aufgebaut (corealis sc.bwpreset). Das FX Grid erlaubt es, komplexe Sidechain-Logik zu bauen: eigene Hüllkurven-Detektoren, Gain-Reduction-Kurven, Sidechain-Routing-Kombinationen die mit Standard-Kompressoren nicht möglich sind. Wann: Wenn Standard-Si'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Farfisa Organ Multisample (Vintage Transistor Orgel)', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Farfisa Organ Multisample (Vintage Transistor Orgel)** [Soul] Quelle: In_Cycles Eine Farfisa SO-Serie Transistor-Orgel wird als Multisample im Sampler abgebildet (Farfisa SO Bass Flute Preset). Die Farfisa hat einen charakteristischen dünnen, nasalen Transistor-Sound ganz anders als Hammond B3. Bass Flute: tiefer Orgelregister mit Flöten-Charakter, ideal für Bass-Linien oder '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Gemischte Drum-Architektur (Poly Grid + E-Hat)', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Gemischte Drum-Architektur (Poly Grid + E-Hat)** [Garage] Quelle: Chee – Hey Now Kicks und Claps werden mit Poly Grid synthetisiert, Hi-Hats und Percussions mit E-Hat. Diese Kombination erlaubt synthetische Kicks/Claps mit präziser Kontrolle, während E-Hat schnelle, leichte Percussion-Variationen bietet. Wann: Wenn der Kick-Charakter sehr spezifisch sein soll (Poly Grid) aber'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Keyboard-Instrument Showcase (Piano, Rhodes, Clav)', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Keyboard-Instrument Showcase (Piano, Rhodes, Clav)** [Pop] Quelle: play-keys Play-Keys enthält mehrere gesampelte Keyboard-Instrumente als Presets: Grand Piano Light (akustisches Klavier), Cosy Sofa Keys (weiche E-Piano-Atmosphäre), Electropiano 1 (klassisches E-Piano), FM Hopepianth (FM-basiertes E-Piano via FM-4), Hybrid Clav (Clavinet via Polymer). Alle lauffähig in Bitwig'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Live-Performance Clip-Launcher Szenenstruktur', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Live-Performance Clip-Launcher Szenenstruktur** [Electronic] Quelle: performance-set Das Performance-Set-Template zeigt die typische Track-Struktur für Live-Auftritte im Clip-Launcher: Kick, Snare, Bass Mel 1/2, Bass Up, Bell, Pad 1/2, Lead, Synth, Reverb-Return, Master. Jede Szene repräsentiert einen Song-Abschnitt (Intro/Build/Drop/Break). Multi-Machine Drums: Legend 606/70'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Multi-Synthesizer Bass Section', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Multi-Synthesizer Bass Section** [Drum and Bass] Quelle: Ferrous Rhythm Der Bass-Bereich besteht nicht aus einem einzigen Synth, sondern aus mehreren spezialisierten Synthesizern in einem Bus: Polymer für Wavetable-Charakter, Phase-4 für Reese-Sounds, ein zweiter Polymer für Sub-Anteile. Jeder Synth spielt das gleiche MIDI aber liefert einen anderen Frequenzbereich oder Chara'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Organisch-Elektronische Hybrid-Komposition', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Organisch-Elektronische Hybrid-Komposition** [Electronica] Quelle: In_Cycles In_Cycles kombiniert echte Acoustic-Drums (Nektar Yamaha Kit), vintage Sampling (SP-12, DustyDrops Loops), analoge Orgel (Farfisa), und synthetische Elemente in einem kohärenten Mix. AddOnChord und AddOnChordHI sind zusätzliche harmonische Schichten. Falko\'s Channel Tool kapselt custom Routing- und F'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Poly Grid Clap / Snare Layering', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Poly Grid Clap / Snare Layering** [Garage] Quelle: Chee – Hey Now Claps und Snares werden im Poly Grid synthetisiert statt mit Samples. Das Clapham-Clap-Preset ist ein bekanntes Garage-Clap-Template. Mehrere Clap-Layers (Rimshot, Snare, Rim Roll, Clap) in separaten Tracks. Wann: UK Garage / Dubstep Percussion-Schichten aufbauen. Synthetische Claps sind dünner und snappiger al'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Poly Grid Kick Synthesis', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Poly Grid Kick Synthesis** [Garage] Quelle: Chee – Hey Now Kicks werden mit dem Poly Grid statt mit E-Kick oder Samples gebaut. Das Preset KickStartR im Poly Grid erlaubt maximale Kontrolle über Pitch-Envelope, Transient-Shape und Sub-Charakter. Wann: Wenn ein Standard-Kick zu generisch klingt oder ein sehr spezifischer Sub-Bass-Charakter mit präziser Hüllkurve gebraucht wird'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Poly Grid Shaker / Perkussion Synthesis', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Poly Grid Shaker / Perkussion Synthesis** [Drum and Bass] Quelle: Ferrous Rhythm Shaker und kleine Percussion-Elemente werden im Poly Grid synthetisiert statt mit Samples. Das \'Grid Shaker 1\'-Preset kombiniert gefilterten Noise mit Hüllkurven für einen lebendigen, nicht-sample-artigen Shaker-Sound. Wann: Wenn gesampelte Shaker zu statisch klingen. Synthetische Shaker lassen s'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Polymer Wavetable Bass', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Polymer Wavetable Bass** [Drum and Bass] Quelle: Ferrous Rhythm Polymer im Wavetable-Mode mit einer custom .wt-Datei (Standard Four.wt) erzeugt einen harmonisch reichen Bass der zwischen verschiedenen Wellenform-Charakteren morphen kann. Wavetable-Scanning per LFO oder Hüllkurve für sich verändernde Oberton-Struktur. Wann: Wenn ein Bass mehr Oberton-Bewegung als ein statische'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Polymerics Pack: Mercury Lead + Gentle Fold Pluck', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Polymerics Pack: Mercury Lead + Gentle Fold Pluck** [Electronic] Quelle: play-synths Das Polymerics-Pack von Bitwig enthält speziell kuratierte Polymer-Presets. Mercury Lead: heller, treibender Lead-Sound mit charakteristischem Bewegungs-charakter. Gentle Fold Pluck: weicher Pluck-Sound mit Faltungs-Waveshaper für sanfte Attack-Transients. Beide sind Wavetable-basiert auf Pol'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:SP-12 / Vintage Sampler Workflow', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **SP-12 / Vintage Sampler Workflow** [Hip-Hop] Quelle: In_Cycles Vintage-Drum-Sounds werden aus dem Goldbaby SP-12 Sample-Pack verwendet (BD_DulVin3_SP12.wav). SP-12 Samples haben einen charakteristischen 12-Bit Lo-Fi Klang mit limitierter Frequenzauflösung. DustyDrops Loops (11 Varianten) werden als Loop-Sampler-Elemente für Texturen und Fills verwendet. Wann: Vintage Hip-Hop '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Sidechain from Bass — Dynamics Preset', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Sidechain from Bass — Dynamics Preset** [Electronic] Quelle: performance-set Das \'Sidechain from Bass\' Preset kapselt einen Kompressor mit voreingestelltem Sidechain-Routing vom Bass-Track. Als sofort ladbares Dynamics-Preset spart es die manuelle Sidechain-Konfiguration. Der Kompressor duckt das Signal wenn der Bass-Track Signal liefert. Wann: Schnelle Sidechain-Kompression '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Synthesizer-Typen Vergleichs-Template', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Synthesizer-Typen Vergleichs-Template** [Electronic] Quelle: play-synths Bitwig\'s Play-Synths-Template enthält je einen Track pro Synthesizer-Paradigma: Polysynth (subtraktiv, Poly Vibrasyn Preset), Phase-4 (Phasenmodulation, Orbital Path), FM-4 (Frequenzmodulation, Next FM Bass), Polymer (Hybrid/Wavetable, Mercury Lead + Gentle Fold Pluck), XY Instrument (Martian Mealtime). '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Tool-Device MIDI Sidechain Ducking', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Tool-Device MIDI Sidechain Ducking** [Garage] Quelle: Chee – Hey Now Das Tool-Device in Bitwig kann einen MIDI-Sidechain-Input empfangen und damit das Audio-Signal ducken. Das \'midi sidechain\'-Preset im Tool nutzt MIDI-Noten vom Kick-Track um den Bass automatisch zu ducken, ohne einen klassischen Kompressor-Sidechain. Wann: Präzises, deterministisches Sidechain-Ducking das ex'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Vocal Chop Multi-Track Stack', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Vocal Chop Multi-Track Stack** [Garage] Quelle: Chee – Hey Now Vocals werden nicht auf einem Track verarbeitet, sondern in mehrere spezialisierte Tracks aufgeteilt: Rohe Vocals, Pitched Vocals, Chopped Vocals, Looped Vox-Layers. Jede Variante hat eigene FX-Kette. Wann: Komplexe Vocal-Arrangements in UK Garage, Grime, Future Bass. Ermöglicht simultanes Abspielen von perkussive'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:World Percussion Sampling (Djembe, Conga, Bongo)', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **World Percussion Sampling (Djembe, Conga, Bongo)** [World] Quelle: play-drums Nicht-westliche Percussion wird mit mehreren Artikulations-Presets gesampelt: Djembe (Left-Hand, Left-Hand Muted, Slap), Conga (Left-Hand, Right-Hand, Slap), Bongo (Sharp High, Sharp Low), Tumba (Left-Hand, Right-Hand), Tambourine (Single Hit), Woodblocks, Shaker. Jede Spieltechnik ist ein separates'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'ProductionPattern:Wurlitzer Multi-Sample Mapping', content: 'Produktions-Muster: **Wurlitzer Multi-Sample Mapping** [Soul] Quelle: play-keys Eine Wurlitzer E-Piano wird mit 5 Velocity-Layers über alle Lagen gesampelt (A0 bis C8, chromatisch). Jede Note hat 5 Velocity-Samples (01-05) für natürliches Anschlagsverhalten. Der Sampler in Bitwig mappt alle 200+ Samples automatisch in eine Multisample-Zone. Wann: Realistischer Wurlitzer-Sound ohne VST-Plugin. 5 Ve'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Accessing Expanded Device View', content: 'Workflow: **Accessing Expanded Device View** Open the Expanded Device View for a device to access its detailed parameters and settings. Wann: When you need to adjust detailed settings of a device in the mixer interface. Schritte: 1. Hover over the device in the mixer interface. | 2. Click the Expanded Device View button. | 3. The Device Panel will open, and the selected device will be displayed in'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Add EQ+ Filters with Mouse Gestures', content: 'Workflow: **Add EQ+ Filters with Mouse Gestures** Quickly add filters to the EQ+ device using mouse gestures. Wann: When you need to shape the frequency response of a track quickly and intuitively. Schritte: 1. Click on the EQ+ device in the device chain. | 2. Double-click at the current mouse cursor position to add a peak filter. | 3. Drag the left or right edge of the EQ curve to add a low-shelf'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adding a Device to a Drum Chain', content: 'Workflow: **Adding a Device to a Drum Chain** How to add a device to a specific drum chain within the Drum Machine. Wann: When you need to add a specific instrument or effect to a drum chain. Schritte: 1. Click on the desired drum chain in the Drum Machine. | 2. Click the Add Device button (+) to load a device into that chain.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adding a Device to a Track', content: 'Workflow: **Adding a Device to a Track** Adds a new device to a track using the Add Device button. Wann: When you need to add a new effect or instrument to a track. Schritte: 1. Click the track\'s Add Device button (the + icon). | 2. Use the Pop-up Browser to select and add the desired device.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adding a Modulator', content: 'Workflow: **Adding a Modulator** Adding a modulator device to a track for modulation control. Wann: To add a modulator device to a track for modulation control. Schritte: 1. Click the modulators button to toggle the Modulators pane to be visible. | 2. Click the Add Modulator button in the modulator slot. | 3. Use the Pop-up Browser to select a modulator device. | 4. Click Ok to place the selected '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adding a Module to the Grid', content: 'Workflow: **Adding a Module to the Grid** Insert a module into the Grid patch for sound design or signal processing. Wann: When you need to add a new module to your modular patch. Schritte: 1. Right-click an unoccupied area of the patch to open the text-menu version of the categories and their modules. | 2. Select the desired module from the menu. | 3. Click on the module to insert it into the pat'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adjust the Arranger Loop Selector', content: 'Workflow: **Adjust the Arranger Loop Selector** Define the region of the Arranger Timeline that will be looped during playback. Wann: To create a repeating section of the Arranger Timeline for continuous playback. Schritte: 1. Click the center of the Arranger Loop Selector and drag it in time to move its position. | 2. Mouse over the left or right edge of the Arranger Loop Selector to change its l'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adjusting Beat Grid Resolution', content: 'Workflow: **Adjusting Beat Grid Resolution** Change the beat grid resolution to align with the desired rhythmic divisions in the Arranger Timeline. Wann: When you need to align clips or events with specific rhythmic divisions for precise arrangement. Schritte: 1. Disable the adaptive beat grid by clicking the adaptive beat grid button or pressing [SLASH]. | 2. Click on the beat grid resolution val'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adjusting Beat Grid Subdivision', content: 'Workflow: **Adjusting Beat Grid Subdivision** Change the rhythmic grouping of the beat grid to use triplets, quintuplets, or septuplets. Wann: When you need to create complex rhythmic patterns or align clips with non-standard subdivisions. Schritte: 1. Disable the adaptive beat grid by clicking the adaptive beat grid button or pressing [SLASH]. | 2. Click on the beat grid subdivision value in the '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adjusting Clip Position', content: 'Workflow: **Adjusting Clip Position** Move a clip to a specific position in the timeline. Wann: When aligning clips with specific rhythmic positions. Schritte: 1. Select the clip in the Arranger. | 2. Adjust the Time parameter to set the desired start position.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adjusting Fade Slopes', content: 'Workflow: **Adjusting Fade Slopes** Adjust the slope of a fade by manipulating its curve. Wann: When fine-tuning transitions between clips or audio segments for smoother or more dynamic fades. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the fade\'s curve. | 2. Hold the [ALT] key and click and drag the mouse up or down to adjust the slope.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adjusting Loop Length of an Arranger Clip', content: 'Workflow: **Adjusting Loop Length of an Arranger Clip** Modify the section of the clip that loops without changing the total length of the clip. Wann: When you want to change how much of a clip repeats without altering its overall duration. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the first repeat marker (dashed vertical line) of the clip until the I-beam cursor with a looping oval appears. | 2. Click and drag in '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adjusting Multiband Compression', content: 'Workflow: **Adjusting Multiband Compression** Fine-tune the compression of individual frequency bands using the Expanded Device View and per-band controls. Wann: When you need precise control over the dynamics of different frequency ranges in a mix. Schritte: 1. Open the Expanded Device View to access individual band displays. | 2. Adjust the Intensity Offset for each band to control the amount of'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Adjusting the Volume Envelope', content: 'Workflow: **Adjusting the Volume Envelope** A workflow for adjusting the volume envelope of an impulse response. Wann: To shape the dynamic range of the impulse response. Schritte: 1. Toggle to the Volume Envelope mode in the Convolution device. | 2. Adjust the start and end gain values using the numeric controls in the Inspector. | 3. Adjust the midpoint\'s timing and gain for more detailed contro'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Animating Played Chords', content: 'Workflow: **Animating Played Chords** Use the Strum device to animate played chords at a steady rate. Wann: For adding rhythmic and dynamic interest to chord progressions. Schritte: 1. Insert the Strum device into your audio track. | 2. Set the Timebase and Rate to control the strumming speed. | 3. Play or trigger chords to hear the animated strumming in real-time.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Apply Tempo Curve to Arranger', content: 'Workflow: **Apply Tempo Curve to Arranger** Writes tempo automation into the project to match beat markers from selected Arranger audio clips. Wann: Use this workflow to ensure audio clips play back neutrally as the project tempo changes. Schritte: 1. Select the desired Arranger audio clips or a time selection of them. | 2. Go to the Tempo menu and choose \'Apply Tempo Curve to Arranger\'. | 3. The '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Applying Fades', content: 'Workflow: **Applying Fades** Apply fade in and fade out effects to an audio clip. Wann: When smoothly transitioning between clips. Schritte: 1. Select the audio clip in the Arranger. | 2. Adjust the Fade parameter to set the desired fade length.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Applying Fades to Audio Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Applying Fades to Audio Clips** A method to create fade in, fade out, or pre-fade effects on audio clips. Wann: When you want to create smooth transitions or adjust the start or end of an audio clip. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the middle of the clip\'s left edge (for fade in) or right edge (for fade out) at the top of the waveform display. | 2. Click and drag the white triangle toward the '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Applying Groove', content: 'Workflow: **Applying Groove** Add rhythmic variation to a clip using groove settings. Wann: When adding swing or shuffle to a clip. Schritte: 1. Select the clip in the Arranger. | 2. Enable the Shuffle parameter to apply Global Groove settings.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Applying Stereo LFO Modulation', content: 'Workflow: **Applying Stereo LFO Modulation** Use LFO modules with phase and offset controls for stereo modulation. Wann: For creating stereo modulation effects with LFOs. Schritte: 1. Insert an LFO module (e.g. S/H LFO, Wavetable LFO). | 2. Adjust the phase parameter to set the starting point of the waveform. | 3. Use the offset control for the right channel to create stereo modulation. | 4. Route'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Assigning a Choke Target', content: 'Workflow: **Assigning a Choke Target** Assign one chain to choke another when triggered, preventing overlapping sounds. Wann: To manage overlapping drum hits or other sounds that should not play simultaneously. Schritte: 1. Right-click the chain you want to trigger the choke. | 2. Select \'Choke targets\' from the context menu. | 3. Choose the chain you want to be choked when this chain is triggered'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Changing Clip Length', content: 'Workflow: **Changing Clip Length** Modify the duration of a clip in the timeline. Wann: When adjusting the length of a clip to match a specific rhythmic pattern. Schritte: 1. Select the clip in the Arranger. | 2. Adjust the Length parameter to set the desired duration.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Changing Track Color', content: 'Workflow: **Changing Track Color** To assign a custom color to a track for visual distinction. Wann: When you want to visually categorize tracks or match them with a color scheme. Schritte: 1. Right-click the track\'s header. | 2. Select a color from the palette in the context menu. | 3. Use the \'x\' icon to clear the color and inherit it from another source, or click the triangle icon to choose a p'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Clearing All Modulation Routings', content: 'Workflow: **Clearing All Modulation Routings** Remove all modulation routings from a source. Wann: When you want to reset all modulation effects from a source. Schritte: 1. Right-click the source\'s modulation routing button. | 2. Select Delete from the context menu.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Clearing a Modulation Routing', content: 'Workflow: **Clearing a Modulation Routing** Remove a specific modulation routing from a source. Wann: When you want to remove a specific modulation effect from a parameter. Schritte: 1. Right-click the source\'s modulation routing button. | 2. Click the x icon to the left of the desired parameter.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Clearing a Modulation Routing from a Target Parameter', content: 'Workflow: **Clearing a Modulation Routing from a Target Parameter** Remove a specific modulation routing from a parameter being controlled. Wann: When you want to remove a specific modulation effect from a parameter. Schritte: 1. Right-click the parameter. | 2. Under the MODULATION ROUTINGS section of the context menu, find the desired modulation source. | 3. Click the x icon at the far right.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Clip Block Navigation', content: 'Workflow: **Clip Block Navigation** Use clip blocks to organize and navigate through groups of clips in the Clip Launcher. Wann: When managing multiple clips in a structured way, such as for drum patterns or sequences. Schritte: 1. Group clips into blocks with blank slots between them. | 2. Use \'First in Block\' or \'Last in Block\' to trigger specific clips within a block. | 3. Use \'Random in Block\''});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Clip Quantization Setup', content: 'Workflow: **Clip Quantization Setup** Configure clip quantization settings to align clip playback with the grid or bar intervals. Wann: When you want clips to start on specific rhythmic or bar intervals for tighter timing. Schritte: 1. Open the Clip Launcher and select the clip you want to configure. | 2. Set the quantization interval to a beat-level (e.g., 1/4) or bar-level (e.g., 1 bar). | 3. Ch'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Closing the Grid Editor', content: 'Workflow: **Closing the Grid Editor** How to close the Grid editor after making changes. Wann: To return to the main interface after editing a Grid patch. Schritte: 1. Double-click on the Grid device\'s overview display within the Device Panel. | 2. Click the x in the top right of the Expanded Device View window.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Comp Recording in the Launcher', content: 'Workflow: **Comp Recording in the Launcher** Record audio in cycle mode, breaking it into takes at a set interval. Wann: When you want to record multiple takes and choose the best parts later. Schritte: 1. Enable Record as Comping Takes from the Play menu. | 2. Set the desired Take Length. | 3. Click a blank slot\'s record button with the audio track armed.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Configure Groove Settings', content: 'Workflow: **Configure Groove Settings** Adjust shuffle and accent parameters to add rhythmic variation to clips. Wann: When you want to add groove and rhythmic interest to your clips. Schritte: 1. Open the Play menu and navigate to the Groove section. | 2. Enable the Shuffle parameter for clips that require rhythmic variation. | 3. Adjust the Shuffle amount to control the intensity of the shuffle '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Configure Playback Settings', content: 'Workflow: **Configure Playback Settings** Adjust metronome, playback ticks, and fill mode settings for precise playback control. Wann: When you need to configure metronome behavior and playback settings. Schritte: 1. Open the Play menu and navigate to the Playback section. | 2. Adjust the Metronome volume to set the desired level for the metronome. | 3. Enable or disable the Play Ticks option to c'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Configuring FX Grid for Note-Based Effects', content: 'Workflow: **Configuring FX Grid for Note-Based Effects** Set up FX Grid to respond to note messages and trigger independent voices. Wann: To create effects that respond to individual notes played on an instrument track. Schritte: 1. Insert an FX Grid device on an audio or instrument track. | 2. Set the Note Source to the appropriate input (e.g., MIDI or audio track). | 3. Enable the Auto-gate opti'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Configuring Monitoring', content: 'Workflow: **Configuring Monitoring** Set up monitoring options for different output paths. Wann: When you need to monitor audio on specific speakers or headphones. Schritte: 1. Go to the Output Monitoring Panel. | 2. Select the desired output path (e.g., 8-inchers). | 3. Enable Solo as Cue to route soloed tracks to headphones.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Configuring Multichannel Audio Interface', content: 'Workflow: **Configuring Multichannel Audio Interface** Set up multiple audio input and output paths for complex monitoring and recording scenarios. Wann: Useful when working with multiple instruments, microphones, or hardware effects units. Schritte: 1. Define input paths for each audio source (e.g., microphone, guitar, synth). | 2. Assign stereo or mono input paths based on the connected devices.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Consolidating Multiple Arranger Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Consolidating Multiple Arranger Clips** Consolidate multiple clips at once to remove unseen data from all of them. Wann: When you need to clean up multiple clips simultaneously. Schritte: 1. Select all the clips you want to consolidate. | 2. Right-click one of the clips and choose \'Consolidate\' from the context menu. | 3. Alternatively, use Edit > Consolidate or [CTRL]+[J] ([CMD]+[J] o'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Consolidating an Arranger Clip', content: 'Workflow: **Consolidating an Arranger Clip** Remove unseen data from a clip to solidify its content. Wann: When you want to finalize a clip and remove any hidden or unused data. Schritte: 1. Right-click the clip and choose \'Consolidate\' from the context menu. | 2. Alternatively, select Edit > Consolidate or press [CTRL]+[J] ([CMD]+[J] on Mac).'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Convert Compressor to Compressor+', content: 'Workflow: **Convert Compressor to Compressor+** Convert a standard Compressor device to Compressor+ for more advanced features. Wann: When you need more control over compression settings and want to use advanced features like Make-up Gain calculation. Schritte: 1. Right-click the Compressor device header. | 2. Select \'Convert to Compressor+\' from the context menu. | 3. The device will load with se'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Copy Modulation Routing', content: 'Workflow: **Copy Modulation Routing** Duplicate modulation routing from one source to another. Wann: To replicate modulation settings across different sources. Schritte: 1. Click on the silver name of the modulation routing. | 2. Drag it onto a different modulation source and release while holding [ALT].'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Copying Clips Between Arranger and Launcher', content: 'Workflow: **Copying Clips Between Arranger and Launcher** Copy clips between the Arranger and Clip Launcher for flexible arrangement and performance. Wann: When you need to move clips between the Arranger and Clip Launcher for editing or performance. Schritte: 1. To copy an Arranger clip to the Launcher: click and drag the clip from the Arranger Timeline to the desired slot on the appropriate trac'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Create Filter Sweeps', content: 'Workflow: **Create Filter Sweeps** Use the LFO and envelope follower to create dynamic filter sweeps and modulation effects. Wann: To add movement and interest to static filter settings. Schritte: 1. Load the Ladder filter device. | 2. Enable the LFO and set its frequency to a suitable value. | 3. Enable the envelope follower and adjust its attack and decay settings. | 4. Modulate the filter\'s cut'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Create Resonant Filter Effects', content: 'Workflow: **Create Resonant Filter Effects** Use the Resonator Bank device to create complex, layered filter effects. Wann: To create rich, evolving filter effects with individual and global control. Schritte: 1. Load the Resonator Bank device. | 2. Adjust the frequency and resonance controls for each individual filter. | 3. Use the global morph controls to blend between different filter settings.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Create Stereo Filter Movement', content: 'Workflow: **Create Stereo Filter Movement** Use the Sweep device to create stereo filter movement and spatial effects. Wann: To create stereo width and movement in filter cutoffs. Schritte: 1. Load the Sweep device. | 2. Adjust the Joint Frequency Control to set the base frequency for both filters. | 3. Enable the Invert option to move filter cutoffs in opposite directions. | 4. Adjust the Stereo '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Create Vocal-Like Effects', content: 'Workflow: **Create Vocal-Like Effects** Use the Vocoder device to impose the timbre of one sound onto another. Wann: To create vocal-like effects or synthesize speech-like textures. Schritte: 1. Load the Vocoder device. | 2. Use the incoming audio signal as the modulator for real-time effects. | 3. Adjust the modulator and carrier chains to shape the timbral characteristics. | 4. Experiment with d'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Create a Filtered Sample', content: 'Workflow: **Create a Filtered Sample** Apply a filter to a sample to shape its tonal characteristics. Wann: For adding character or shaping the frequency content of a sample. Schritte: 1. Load a sample into the Sampler. | 2. Adjust the filter mode to select the desired filter type. | 3. Set the cutoff frequency to control the filter\'s starting point. | 4. Adjust the resonance to emphasize specific'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Create a Modulation Effect with LFO', content: 'Workflow: **Create a Modulation Effect with LFO** Use an LFO to modulate a filter or effect parameter for dynamic movement. Wann: To add rhythmic or evolving modulation to a sound. Schritte: 1. Insert an LFO device into the modulation chain. | 2. Connect the LFO output to the parameter you want to modulate (e.g., filter cutoff). | 3. Adjust the Rate and Timebase to set the modulation speed. | 4. U'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Create a Polyphonic Instrument with Poly Grid', content: 'Workflow: **Create a Polyphonic Instrument with Poly Grid** Use the Poly Grid to create a polyphonic instrument with multiple voices. Wann: For building complex polyphonic instruments with layered sounds. Schritte: 1. Open the Poly Grid device. | 2. Add multiple instruments or samples to the grid. | 3. Use voice management to control the number of active voices. | 4. Use modulation sources to crea'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating Algorithmic Variations', content: 'Workflow: **Creating Algorithmic Variations** Use Bar Sync or Random to generate algorithmic variations of note clips. Wann: For creating dynamic and evolving musical patterns. Schritte: 1. Insert the Bar Sync or Random device into your audio track. | 2. Adjust the settings to control the variation type (reproducible or random). | 3. Play or trigger notes to hear the algorithmic variations in real'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating Crossfades', content: 'Workflow: **Creating Crossfades** A method to create crossfades between overlapping audio clips. Wann: When you want to smoothly transition between overlapping audio clips. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the middle of the intersection of the clips at the top of the waveform display. | 2. Click the white triangle where you want the crossfade boundary to be and drag across the clips\' border. | 3. Release t'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating Feedback with Long Delay', content: 'Workflow: **Creating Feedback with Long Delay** A method to create feedback loops in The Grid by using the Long Delay module. Wann: When attempting to create feedback loops that would otherwise be blocked by The Grid\'s restrictions. Schritte: 1. Identify the feedback path where you want to create a loop. | 2. Insert a Long Delay (Delay/FX) module into the feedback path. | 3. Connect the output of '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating Looping Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Creating Looping Clips** Set up a clip to loop continuously. Wann: When creating repeating patterns or sequences. Schritte: 1. Select the clip in the Arranger. | 2. Enable the Loop parameter to toggle looping on.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating Parallel Device Chains', content: 'Workflow: **Creating Parallel Device Chains** Use container devices to route signals to multiple devices simultaneously. Wann: When you want to apply multiple effects or instruments to the same signal. Schritte: 1. Insert a container device (e.g., FX Layer or Instrument Layer) into the track. | 2. Add multiple devices to the container for parallel processing. | 3. Use the Mix parameter on each dev'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating Stereo Oscillator Effects', content: 'Workflow: **Creating Stereo Oscillator Effects** Use oscillator modules with frequency offset inversion for stereo pitch modulation. Wann: For creating stereo oscillator effects with phase or frequency modulation. Schritte: 1. Insert an oscillator module (e.g. Pulse, Sawtooth). | 2. Adjust the frequency offset value. | 3. Click the polarity signal (±/∓) to invert the offset for the right channel. '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating Vibrato with LFO', content: 'Workflow: **Creating Vibrato with LFO** Use an LFO to modulate the pitch parameter for a vibrato effect. Wann: To add vibrato to the overall pitch of the instrument. Schritte: 1. Create an LFO device and set its waveform to a sine wave. | 2. Route the LFO output to the Pitch parameter of the Phase-4 instrument. | 3. Adjust the LFO rate and depth to achieve the desired vibrato effect.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating a 90s-Style Delay', content: 'Workflow: **Creating a 90s-Style Delay** Use Note FX to create a 90s-style delay effect. Wann: For adding retro delay effects to your tracks. Schritte: 1. Insert the Note FX device into your audio track. | 2. Adjust the settings to create a delay-like effect. | 3. Play or trigger notes to hear the delay effect in real-time.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating a Custom Envelope with Segments', content: 'Workflow: **Creating a Custom Envelope with Segments** Draw and edit a custom envelope shape using the Curve Editor. Wann: For creating unique envelope shapes with precise control over attack, decay, sustain, and release stages. Schritte: 1. Open the Segments device in Bitwig Studio. | 2. Use the Curve Editor to draw the desired envelope shape. | 3. Set the Rate and Timebase parameters to control '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating a One-Shot Arpeggiator', content: 'Workflow: **Creating a One-Shot Arpeggiator** Use the Strum device to create a one-shot arpeggiator that runs up or down the played notes once. Wann: For adding rhythmic and dynamic interest to chord progressions. Schritte: 1. Insert the Strum device into your audio track. | 2. Set the Number of Steps to create a pattern of up and down strum directions. | 3. Play or trigger chords to hear the one-'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating a Smart Moving Quantize', content: 'Workflow: **Creating a Smart Moving Quantize** Use the Smart Moving Quantize device to take your playing and spread each note to this beat or the next. Wann: For adding rhythmic and dynamic interest to your playing. Schritte: 1. Insert the Smart Moving Quantize device into your audio track. | 2. Adjust the settings to control the quantization behavior. | 3. Play or trigger notes to hear the quanti'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating a Step Sequencer Pattern', content: 'Workflow: **Creating a Step Sequencer Pattern** Create a rhythmic pattern using the Steps device. Wann: Use when you want to design a tempo-synced modulation pattern. Schritte: 1. Open the Steps device and set the step count to the desired number of steps. | 2. Set the direction to \'Forward\' or \'Ping-Pong\' for rhythmic variation. | 3. Adjust the polarity to \'Bipolar\' for dynamic modulation effects'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating a Thick Lead Sound', content: 'Workflow: **Creating a Thick Lead Sound** Use the Polysynth to create a rich, layered lead sound with detuned oscillators and filter modulation. Wann: When designing a lead synth sound with depth and movement. Schritte: 1. Set the Unison knob to a value between 2v and 16v to add multiple voices. | 2. Adjust the detuning using the Unison knob to create a chorused effect. | 3. Use the Shape control '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating a Track', content: 'Workflow: **Creating a Track** Adding a new track to a project. Wann: When additional tracks are needed for recording or processing. Schritte: 1. Go to the Add menu and select either Add Instrument Track, Add Audio Track, Add FX Track, or Add Group Track.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating a Voice Stack Pattern', content: 'Workflow: **Creating a Voice Stack Pattern** Create a unique modulation pattern using the Stack Spread device with a voice stack. Wann: Use when designing layered sounds with varied modulation across multiple voices. Schritte: 1. Enable Voice Stacking on the parent device. | 2. Add the Stack Spread device to the modulation chain. | 3. Select a spread mode (e.g., \'Flipped\' or \'Rand+\') for modulatio'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating an Instrument Layer', content: 'Workflow: **Creating an Instrument Layer** Set up multiple instruments to play simultaneously using the Instrument Layer device. Wann: To create layered sounds or combine multiple instruments into one track. Schritte: 1. Add the Instrument Layer device to your track. | 2. Click the \'Add Device\' button to create new instrument chains. | 3. Assign instruments to each chain. | 4. Use the Inspector Pa'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Creating and Using Cue Markers', content: 'Workflow: **Creating and Using Cue Markers** Create and use cue markers to quickly navigate and trigger playback from specific points in your arrangement. Wann: When you need to jump to specific sections of your arrangement for playback or editing. Schritte: 1. Right-click within the Beat Ruler and enable Show Cue Markers from the context menu. | 2. Right-click the Beat Ruler and select Insert Cue'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Cycle Recording in the Arranger', content: 'Workflow: **Cycle Recording in the Arranger** A workflow to record audio or MIDI within a defined loop section for comping. Wann: When you want to record multiple takes within a looped section for comping or refining audio. Schritte: 1. Enable the Arranger Loop toggle with the desired period set. | 2. Enable the track\'s record arm button. | 3. Enable the Global Record button. | 4. Activate the tra'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Deactivating and Reactivating a Track', content: 'Workflow: **Deactivating and Reactivating a Track** To temporarily disable a track to reduce CPU usage or silence it. Wann: When you need to free up CPU resources or focus on specific tracks. Schritte: 1. Right-click the track\'s header. | 2. Choose \'Activate/Deactivate Track\' from the context menu, or press [ALT]+[A].'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Deleting a Browser Snapshot', content: 'Workflow: **Deleting a Browser Snapshot** Remove a saved browser search configuration from the list. Wann: When you no longer need a specific snapshot and want to clean up your browser settings. Schritte: 1. Click the magnifying glass icon in the search bar. | 2. Click the \'x\' icon to the right of the snapshot\'s name.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Deleting a Module', content: 'Workflow: **Deleting a Module** Remove a module from The Grid and automatically reroute signals. Wann: When simplifying a patch or removing unnecessary components. Schritte: 1. Select the module in the Grid editor. | 2. Press [DELETE] or [BACKSPACE].'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Design a Custom LFO Curve', content: 'Workflow: **Design a Custom LFO Curve** Create a custom LFO waveform using the Curve Editor. Wann: To generate unique modulation patterns for sound design. Schritte: 1. Insert the Curves LFO device into the modulation chain. | 2. Open the Curve Editor to draw or load a BWCURVE file. | 3. Adjust the curve segments to shape the LFO waveform. | 4. Use the Rate and Timebase controls to set the modulat'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Design an Electronic Tom Sound with E-Tom', content: 'Workflow: **Design an Electronic Tom Sound with E-Tom** Create an electronic tom sound using the E-Tom instrument. Wann: For synthesizing tom drum sounds with pitch modulation. Schritte: 1. Open the E-Tom instrument. | 2. Adjust the GEN section to control the oscillator parameters. | 3. Use the pitch modulation feature to add variation to the tom sound. | 4. Experiment with different settings to a'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Design an Envelope for a Sample', content: 'Workflow: **Design an Envelope for a Sample** Create an amplitude envelope to shape the attack, decay, sustain, and release of a sample. Wann: For adding dynamic and expressive qualities to a sample. Schritte: 1. Load a sample into the Sampler. | 2. Set the envelope mode to AHDSR. | 3. Adjust the attack time to control the initial fade-in. | 4. Set the decay time to control the fade from the attac'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Detect Tempo in Audio Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Detect Tempo in Audio Clips** Automatically detects the tempo and beat structure of an audio clip. Wann: When you need to synchronize audio with the project\'s tempo. Schritte: 1. Select the audio clip you want to analyze. | 2. Go to the Tempo > Detect Event Tempo… function. | 3. Set the Tempo Range and choose the Algorithm (Assume Fixed Tempo or Detect Tempo Changes).'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Disabling Sends', content: 'Workflow: **Disabling Sends** Toggle individual or all sends on a track to bypass routing without losing level settings. Wann: To save CPU resources or simplify the mixer interface. Schritte: 1. Click on the name of the send to toggle it on or off. | 2. Use [SHIFT]-click on any send of the track to toggle all sends on or off.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Disabling Voice Solo', content: 'Workflow: **Disabling Voice Solo** Disables the voice solo mode in an active voice stack to hear all voices simultaneously. Wann: When you want to disable solo mode in a voice stack to hear all voices at once. Schritte: 1. Click the voice solo menu (S). | 2. Select the \'Disable Solo\' option at the top of the list.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Dubstep Half-Time Drums', content: 'Workflow: **Dubstep Half-Time Drums** Half-Time Drum-Pattern für Dark Dubstep Schritte: 1. v9 Kick auf Kick-Pad (MIDI 36): Tune -18, Punch 0.8, Decay 0.5s | 2. v9 Snare auf Snare-Pad (MIDI 38): Beat 3 (half-time), Reverb 1.5s | 3. v9 Hat Closed auf HiHat-Pad (MIDI 42): 8tel-Noten, vel 60/100 alternierend | 4. Sidechain: Compressor auf Bass-Track, Input=Kick | 5. Sidechain: Threshold -20dB, Ratio 6'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Dubstep Reese Bass', content: 'Workflow: **Dubstep Reese Bass** Klassischer Reese-Bass für Dubstep Schritte: 1. FM-4 auf Bass-Track laden | 2. Algorithmus: Op1 moduliert Op2 (Kette) | 3. Op1 Ratio: 1.0, Op2 Ratio: 1.5 | 4. Op1 Detune: +8 Cents | 5. Distortion: Mode=Fold, Drive=0.4 | 6. Ladder-Filter: Cutoff 800Hz, Resonance 0.3 | 7. Compressor: Threshold -18dB, Ratio 4:1, Attack 2ms'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Editing Curves in the Curve Editor', content: 'Workflow: **Editing Curves in the Curve Editor** A workflow for creating and modifying curves using the Curve Editor in Bitwig Studio. Wann: When you need to design custom modulation curves for devices or modulators. Schritte: 1. Click on a curve display to open the Curve Editor in a resizable window. | 2. Use the available tools (Pointer, Pencil, Step, Half Step, Saw Up, Saw Down, Triangle) to dr'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Editing Modulation Data with the Curve Editor', content: 'Workflow: **Editing Modulation Data with the Curve Editor** Edit curve-based modulation data using the Curve Editor. Wann: When you need to create or modify complex modulation curves for devices that support BWCURVE files. Schritte: 1. Open the Pop-out Editor for the modulator or module that contains editable playback data. | 2. Use the Curve Editor to draw and edit the modulation curve as needed.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Editing Multisample Zones', content: 'Workflow: **Editing Multisample Zones** A workflow for editing and managing zones in a multisample, including adjusting key ranges, velocity ranges, and select parameter ranges. Wann: Useful when working with multisamples and needing precise control over each zone\'s playback parameters. Schritte: 1. Open the Sampler device and select the multisample you want to edit. | 2. Switch to the list editor'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Editing Time Selections', content: 'Workflow: **Editing Time Selections** Make time selections by clicking in the bottom portion of an Arranger clip and use keyboard shortcuts to navigate events. Wann: When editing specific events within note or audio clips. Schritte: 1. Click in the bottom portion of an Arranger clip to make a time selection. | 2. Use keyboard shortcuts to move between relevant events such as note start/end positio'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Filter Envelope Setup', content: 'Workflow: **Filter Envelope Setup** Configure the filter envelope generator (FEG) to shape the filter\'s response over time. Wann: To add dynamic movement to the filter\'s cutoff frequency. Schritte: 1. Access the FEG section in the Polymer device. | 2. Set the Attack, Decay, Sustain, and Release controls to define the envelope shape. | 3. Adjust the velocity scaling control to control how much note'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Focusing on a Track\'s Device', content: 'Workflow: **Focusing on a Track\'s Device** Opens the Device Panel to focus on a specific device within a track. Wann: When you need to edit or view the parameters of a specific device in detail. Schritte: 1. Double-click the device in the Devices Section.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Fold Audio Clips into Takes', content: 'Workflow: **Fold Audio Clips into Takes** Breaks an audio clip into multiple takes for detailed editing. Wann: When you need to edit individual takes of an audio clip. Schritte: 1. Select the audio clip you want to fold. | 2. Go to the Slice / Fold > Fold to Takes… function. | 3. Set the number of takes or the length of each take.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Free Scaling of Arranger Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Free Scaling of Arranger Clips** Scale a clip\'s length freely without affecting its content. Wann: When you want to stretch or compress a clip\'s duration without altering its internal structure. Schritte: 1. Hold [ALT] and click and drag from the left or right edge of the clip. | 2. Dragging the right edge anchors the left edge, and vice versa.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Free Scaling of Time Selections', content: 'Workflow: **Free Scaling of Time Selections** Stretch or compress a time selection, affecting clips and events within it. Wann: When you need to adjust the timing of multiple clips or events in a specific time range. Schritte: 1. Make a time selection by clicking and dragging in the timeline. | 2. Hold [ALT] and click and drag from the left or right boundary of the selection. | 3. This will stretc'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Generate a Seed Value for a Clip', content: 'Workflow: **Generate a Seed Value for a Clip** Create a consistent randomized pattern for a clip by setting a Seed value. Wann: When you want to produce the same randomized pattern every time a clip is triggered. Schritte: 1. Click on the right side of the Seed field in the clip settings. | 2. A visualization of the current Seed value is shown. | 3. Play the clip to hear the pattern produced by th'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Generating One-Shot Timbre/Pan Envelopes', content: 'Workflow: **Generating One-Shot Timbre/Pan Envelopes** Use Room Spatialization with maximum Damping to create one-shot timbre and pan envelopes. Wann: For creating unique spatial effects and dynamic timbral changes. Schritte: 1. Insert the Room Spatialization device into your audio track. | 2. Set Room Spatialization to maximum damping. | 3. Trigger notes to hear the one-shot envelopes in real-tim'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Grouping Tracks', content: 'Workflow: **Grouping Tracks** Grouping multiple tracks into a single track for easier management. Wann: When managing multiple tracks as a single unit. Schritte: 1. Select the tracks you wish to group. | 2. Press [CTRL]+[G] ([CMD]+[G] on Mac).'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Inserting Arranger Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Inserting Arranger Clips** This workflow outlines the process of inserting and working with Arranger Clips using the Browser and Arranger Timeline Panel. Wann: When starting a new project or adding content to the Arranger, this workflow helps in efficiently inserting and arranging clips. Schritte: 1. Open the Browser and select the \'Samples + Clips\' source. | 2. Search or filter for th'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Inserting Clips from Browser', content: 'Workflow: **Inserting Clips from Browser** Insert a clip into the Arranger Timeline by dragging it from the Browser Panel to the desired track position. Wann: When you need to add a clip to a specific track in the timeline. Schritte: 1. Click and drag the clip from the Browser Panel to the desired timeline position on the appropriate track.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Inserting Clips on New Tracks', content: 'Workflow: **Inserting Clips on New Tracks** Insert a clip on a brand new Arranger track by dragging it from the Browser Panel between existing tracks. Wann: When you need to add a clip to a new track in the timeline. Schritte: 1. Click and drag the clip from the Browser Panel to the desired timeline position between existing tracks.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Inserting Time Signature Changes', content: 'Workflow: **Inserting Time Signature Changes** Insert time signature changes to define different rhythmic structures in your arrangement. Wann: When you need to change the time signature at specific points in your arrangement. Schritte: 1. Right-click the Beat Ruler and select Insert Time Signature Change. | 2. Click the orange triangle or name of the time signature change to select it. | 3. Move '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Layering a Device', content: 'Workflow: **Layering a Device** Layers a device on top of another device within the track\'s device chain. Wann: When you want to apply the same effect to multiple signals or create parallel processing. Schritte: 1. Hold [SHIFT] and click and drag the device over the device where the layer should be inserted.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Lengthening an Arranger Clip', content: 'Workflow: **Lengthening an Arranger Clip** Extend the length of an Arranger clip without losing its original content. Wann: When you want to make a clip longer while preserving its original content for later use. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the top right edge of the clip until a half-bracket cursor appears. | 2. Click and drag to the right to extend the clip.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Loading an Impulse Response', content: 'Workflow: **Loading an Impulse Response** A workflow for loading an audio file as an impulse response for the Convolution device. Wann: To create a custom reverb effect using a real-world impulse response. Schritte: 1. Drag an audio file from Bitwig\'s browser or the OS\'s file manager onto the Convolution device. | 2. The first 45 seconds of the audio file will be loaded as an impulse response. | 3'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Loading an Instrument Preset', content: 'Workflow: **Loading an Instrument Preset** A process to load an instrument device into the Arrangement Timeline Panel to realize note clips. Wann: Before recording note clips to ensure the notes are interpreted by an instrument. Schritte: 1. Go to the Browser Panel. | 2. Select the Bitwig Presets source. | 3. Browse to the Synth category or any category with playable presets. | 4. Drag a preset in'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Loading and Using Devices', content: 'Workflow: **Loading and Using Devices** Load and use devices on a track to process audio or generate sound. Wann: When you need to apply effects or instruments to a track. Schritte: 1. Open the Device Panel. | 2. Select a device from the list (e.g., Oscilloscope, Blur, E-Clap). | 3. Drag the device onto the track\'s device chain. | 4. Adjust the device parameters to achieve the desired effect.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Locking the Grid Editor', content: 'Workflow: **Locking the Grid Editor** To prevent accidental changes to the Grid layout while adjusting parameters. Wann: When you want to focus on parameter adjustments without modifying the module layout. Schritte: 1. Click the padlock icon in the Grid editor. | 2. This hides the module palette and removes background lines for a cleaner interface.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Looping an Arranger Clip', content: 'Workflow: **Looping an Arranger Clip** Create a loop from an Arranger clip by extending its length. Wann: When you want to repeat a musical idea or pattern for a longer duration. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the bottom right edge of the clip until the half-bracket cursor with a looping oval appears. | 2. Click and drag to the right to extend the clip beyond its full length. | 3. Additional copies of th'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Managing Modulators', content: 'Workflow: **Managing Modulators** Managing modulator devices in the Modulators pane. Wann: To manage modulator devices in the Modulators pane. Schritte: 1. Right-click the background of the modulator slot to access the context menu. | 2. Use the context menu to cut, copy, paste, duplicate, or delete a modulator. | 3. Use the context menu to toggle modulators as Active or not.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Manipulating Stereo Signals with Mix Modules', content: 'Workflow: **Manipulating Stereo Signals with Mix Modules** Use stereo split and merge modules to manipulate stereo signals. Wann: For separating or reconstructing stereo signals into left-right or mid-side formats. Schritte: 1. Insert a Stereo Split module. | 2. Route the signal to the left and right channels. | 3. Use a Stereo Merge module to reconstruct the signal. | 4. Adjust the level controls'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Mastering Chain', content: 'Workflow: **Mastering Chain** Standard Mastering-Kette für elektronische Musik Schritte: 1. EQ-5: Korrektur-EQ, HPF bei 30Hz | 2. Compressor: Threshold -18dB, Ratio 2:1, soft knee | 3. Saturator: Drive 0.15 für Wärme | 4. EQ-5: High Shelf +1.5dB bei 8kHz | 5. Limiter: Ceiling -0.3 dBTP, Lookahead 1ms | 6. Ziel: -14 LUFS für Streaming'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Merging Signals with a Module', content: 'Workflow: **Merging Signals with a Module** Merge a preexisting signal with a newly created patch cord using a module. Wann: When you need to combine multiple signals into one using a module like a Mixer. Schritte: 1. Draw the cord from the desired out port to the occupied in port. | 2. Hold one of the available modifiers listed in the window footer.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Modulate Sample Playback', content: 'Workflow: **Modulate Sample Playback** Use the Sampler\'s modulation controls to dynamically change the playback of a sample. Wann: For creating dynamic and evolving sample-based sounds. Schritte: 1. Load a sample into the Sampler. | 2. Enable the RAM chip icon to load the sample into memory. | 3. Use the PLAY parameter to modulate the sample start time. | 4. Use the LOOP parameter to modulate the '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Modulating Filter Cutoff with FEG', content: 'Workflow: **Modulating Filter Cutoff with FEG** Use the FEG in Polysynth to modulate the filter cutoff frequency and route it to other parameters for dynamic sound changes. Wann: When creating evolving filter effects or dynamic sound textures. Schritte: 1. Open the Polysynth device. | 2. Locate the FEG (Filter Envelope Generator) section. | 3. Enable the modulation routing button on the FEG. | 4. '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Modulating Multiple Parameters with a Modulator', content: 'Workflow: **Modulating Multiple Parameters with a Modulator** Connect a modulation source to multiple parameters to create dynamic and expressive sound changes. Wann: When you want to modulate multiple parameters simultaneously, such as filter cutoff and resonance with an LFO. Schritte: 1. Enable the modulation routing button of the modulation source. | 2. Click the target parameter and drag its v'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Modulating Volume with AEG', content: 'Workflow: **Modulating Volume with AEG** Use the AEG in Polysynth to modulate the volume amplifier and route it to other parameters for dynamic volume changes. Wann: When creating dynamic volume changes or amplitude modulation effects. Schritte: 1. Open the Polysynth device. | 2. Locate the AEG (Amplitude Envelope Generator) section. | 3. Enable the modulation routing button on the AEG. | 4. Route'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Modulation Order Control', content: 'Workflow: **Modulation Order Control** Adjust the order of phase distortion and phase modulation using the directional arrow between SHAPE and MOD parameters. Wann: To determine whether phase distortion is applied before or after phase modulation. Schritte: 1. Locate the arrow between the SHAPE and MOD parameters. | 2. Click on the arrow to rotate it and change the modulation order. | 3. Test the '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Move Modulation Routing', content: 'Workflow: **Move Modulation Routing** Transfer modulation routing from one source to another. Wann: To reassign modulation sources for more flexible control. Schritte: 1. Click on the silver name of the modulation routing. | 2. Drag it onto a different modulation source and release.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Move or Copy All Modulation Routings', content: 'Workflow: **Move or Copy All Modulation Routings** Transfer or duplicate all modulation routings from a specific source. Wann: To apply a complete modulation setup to another source. Schritte: 1. Click on the source\'s title or modulation routing button. | 2. Use drag-and-drop actions with available options in the window footer or Device Panel.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Move the Play Start Marker', content: 'Workflow: **Move the Play Start Marker** Set the starting point for playback in the Arranger Timeline. Wann: To define where playback should begin in the Arranger Timeline. Schritte: 1. Single-click in the top half of the Beat Ruler. | 2. Alternatively, single-click anywhere within the Arranger Timeline with the Pointer tool. | 3. Click and drag the play position in the window header\'s display sec'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Moving Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Moving Clips** Move a clip within the Arranger Timeline Panel by clicking and dragging it with the mouse. Wann: When you need to reposition a clip in the timeline. Schritte: 1. Click and drag the clip with the mouse to move it within the Arranger Timeline Panel.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Moving Tracks', content: 'Workflow: **Moving Tracks** Reordering tracks in the Arranger. Wann: When adjusting the order of tracks for better organization or mixing. Schritte: 1. Click and drag the track\'s header vertically to move it.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Moving a Device', content: 'Workflow: **Moving a Device** Moves a device to a different position within the track\'s device chain. Wann: When you need to reorder devices for better signal flow or processing order. Schritte: 1. Click and drag the device to the desired location. | 2. Hold [ALT] to copy the device instead of moving it.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Muting Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Muting Clips** Disable a clip from playing without removing it. Wann: When temporarily disabling a clip in a complex arrangement. Schritte: 1. Select the clip in the Arranger. | 2. Toggle the Mute parameter to disable playback.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Navigating Clips with Keyboard Shortcuts', content: 'Workflow: **Navigating Clips with Keyboard Shortcuts** Use keyboard shortcuts to navigate and edit clips in the Arranger Timeline Panel. Wann: When making precise edits to clip positions and loop markers. Schritte: 1. Press [LEFT ARROW] or [RIGHT ARROW] to select the previous or next clip. | 2. Press [SHIFT]+[LEFT ARROW] or [SHIFT]+[RIGHT ARROW] to extend the current selection to include the next '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Navigating Drum Chains', content: 'Workflow: **Navigating Drum Chains** How to navigate through the 128 available drum chains in the Drum Machine. Wann: When working with multiple drum chains that are not all visible at once. Schritte: 1. Use the chain scroll area on the left to click or scroll through different sets of chains.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Navigating Filters', content: 'Workflow: **Navigating Filters** Use keyboard shortcuts to move between filters in the Browser Panel. Wann: To efficiently navigate through available filters without using the mouse. Schritte: 1. Press [CTRL]+[ALT]+[UP ARROW] or [CTRL]+[ALT]+[DOWN ARROW] (Mac: [CMD]+[ALT]+[UP ARROW] or [CMD]+[ALT]+[DOWN ARROW]) to move between filters in the vertical Browser Panel. | 2. In the horizontal Pop-up Br'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Navigating Group Tracks in the Arranger', content: 'Workflow: **Navigating Group Tracks in the Arranger** Switch between different levels of the project hierarchy and view the contents of group tracks or their master tracks. Wann: When organizing and editing clips within group tracks in a project. Schritte: 1. Click the project navigation menu to expose the hierarchy of the current project. | 2. Select a group track to change the context displayed '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Next Action Configuration', content: 'Workflow: **Next Action Configuration** Set up a Next Action to define what happens after a clip has played for a certain amount of time. Wann: When you want to automate transitions between clips or trigger specific actions after a clip finishes. Schritte: 1. Open the Next Action section in the Clip Launcher. | 2. Select the desired Next Action Function from the drop-down menu. | 3. Enable the \'En'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Normalize Audio Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Normalize Audio Clips** Makes non-destructive level adjustments to selected audio clips to bring them close to 0 dB. Wann: Use this workflow to ensure consistent volume levels across multiple audio clips. Schritte: 1. Select the audio clips you want to normalize. | 2. Go to the Clip menu and choose \'Normalize\'.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Opening the Grid Editor', content: 'Workflow: **Opening the Grid Editor** How to open and interact with the Grid editor in Bitwig Studio. Wann: To edit or create a Grid patch. Schritte: 1. Click on the Grid device\'s overview display within the Device Panel to open the Grid editor.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Play the Arranger Timeline', content: 'Workflow: **Play the Arranger Timeline** Start playback of the Arranger timeline with various options for starting points. Wann: To play back the Arranger timeline from different positions. Schritte: 1. Press [SPACE BAR] or [P] to engage the transport and start playback. | 2. Press [ALT]+[SPACE BAR] or [ALT]+[P] to play from the beginning. | 3. Press [SHIFT]+[SPACE BAR] or [SHIFT]+[P] to play from'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Quantize Audio Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Quantize Audio Clips** Aligns audio events with the beat grid for rhythmic precision. Wann: When you need to align audio events precisely with the beat grid. Schritte: 1. Select the audio clip you want to quantize. | 2. Go to the Quantize > Quantize Audio… function. | 3. Adjust the parameters as needed for the desired alignment. | 4. Apply the quantization to align the audio with the b'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Quick Slice Mode', content: 'Workflow: **Quick Slice Mode** A method to insert successive cuts in a clip without snapping to the beat grid. Wann: When you need to place cuts freely without snapping to the beat grid. Schritte: 1. Hold [SHIFT]+[ALT] and click to initiate Quick Slice mode without quantization. | 2. Drag either to the right or left to insert successive cuts.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Recalling a Browser Snapshot', content: 'Workflow: **Recalling a Browser Snapshot** Restore a previously saved browser search configuration. Wann: When you need to return to a specific search setup quickly. Schritte: 1. Click the magnifying glass icon in the search bar. | 2. Click the name of the snapshot or the play triangle icon next to it.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Record Launcher Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Record Launcher Clips** Record new Launcher clips or print results from the Launcher. Wann: When you want to capture audio or MIDI clips for later use. Schritte: 1. Enable the track\'s record arm button. | 2. Click a blank slot\'s record button. | 3. Begin playing notes or recording audio.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Record to Arranger Timeline', content: 'Workflow: **Record to Arranger Timeline** Record triggered Launcher clips directly into the Arranger timeline. Wann: When you want to capture improvisations or performances for editing. Schritte: 1. Enable the Global Record button. | 2. Activate the transport. | 3. Trigger the clips or scenes from the Launcher.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Recording Clips in the Arranger', content: 'Workflow: **Recording Clips in the Arranger** Record new note and audio clips in the Arranger after setting up your audio and MIDI interfaces. Wann: When you are ready to record new material into your arrangement. Schritte: 1. Ensure your audio and MIDI interfaces/controllers are set up properly (see section 0.2.2). | 2. Prepare your tracks for recording by routing the correct signals. | 3. Use th'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Recording Clips in the Clip Launcher', content: 'Workflow: **Recording Clips in the Clip Launcher** Record clips directly into the Clip Launcher by enabling record mode and triggering the slot record button. Wann: When you want to record new clips or overdub existing ones in the Clip Launcher. Schritte: 1. Enable record mode in the Play menu. | 2. Click the slot record button in an empty launcher slot. | 3. Trigger the clip or scene to start rec'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Recording Comping Takes', content: 'Workflow: **Recording Comping Takes** Record new comping takes into a looping Arranger clip. Wann: When you want to record multiple takes and consolidate them into a single clip. Schritte: 1. Use the Consolidate function for the relevant portion of the clip before recording. | 2. Record the new comping take into the looping Arranger clip.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Recording Note Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Recording Note Clips** A process to record note clips by setting up a sound source and MIDI source. Wann: To record MIDI notes that will be interpreted by an instrument device. Schritte: 1. Ensure a sound source is available for audio. | 2. Ensure a MIDI source is available for recording notes. | 3. Hit record to start recording the note clip.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Recording an Arranger Audio Clip', content: 'Workflow: **Recording an Arranger Audio Clip** A workflow to record audio into an Arranger clip. Wann: When you want to record audio directly into a clip for use in the Arranger timeline. Schritte: 1. Set the desired input source in the track\'s input chooser. | 2. Enable the track\'s record arm button. | 3. Enable the Global Record button. | 4. Activate the transport to begin recording.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Recording an Arranger Note Clip', content: 'Workflow: **Recording an Arranger Note Clip** A workflow to record MIDI notes into an Arranger clip. Wann: When you want to record MIDI notes into a clip for use in the Arranger timeline. Schritte: 1. Enable the track\'s record arm button. | 2. Enable the Global Record button. | 3. Activate the transport and begin playing notes.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Releasing a Launcher Clip Normally', content: 'Workflow: **Releasing a Launcher Clip Normally** Release a Launcher clip by letting go of the mouse button or controller pad used to trigger it. Wann: When you want to execute the Main Release Action immediately. Schritte: 1. Release the mouse button or controller pad used to trigger the clip.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Releasing a Launcher Clip with Alternate Behavior', content: 'Workflow: **Releasing a Launcher Clip with Alternate Behavior** Release a Launcher clip using its alternate (ALT) release behavior. Wann: When you want to execute the ALT Release Action immediately. Schritte: 1. Hold the [ALT] key and release the mouse button used to trigger the clip. | 2. Alternatively, hold the controller\'s [SHIFT] button and release the pad used to trigger the clip.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Reloading Crashed Plug-ins', content: 'Workflow: **Reloading Crashed Plug-ins** Reloads crashed plug-ins to restore their functionality without interrupting audio playback. Wann: When a plug-in crashes and needs to be reloaded. Schritte: 1. Click the \'Reload Plug-in\' button in the Device Panel to reload a specific crashed plug-in. | 2. Click \'Reload All Plug-ins\' to reload every crashed plug-in and leave those that haven\'t crashed alon'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Renaming a Device', content: 'Workflow: **Renaming a Device** Change the name of a device for better organization and clarity. Wann: When working with multiple similar devices or for easier identification. Schritte: 1. Select the device in the chain. | 2. Open the Inspector Panel. | 3. Type a new name into the device name field.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Renaming a Track', content: 'Workflow: **Renaming a Track** To rename a track for better organization or clarity. Wann: When you want to customize track names for easier identification in the Arrange view. Schritte: 1. Right-click the track\'s header. | 2. Choose \'Rename\' from the context menu. | 3. Enter the new name and press Enter.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Reordering Modules', content: 'Workflow: **Reordering Modules** Reorder modules within a patch by dragging and dropping them onto a desired port. Wann: When you need to rearrange the order of modules in your patch for better organization or signal flow. Schritte: 1. Drag the module from its current position onto the port where you want it connected. | 2. Release the click/touch to reroute the module within the patch.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Replacing a Module in The Grid', content: 'Workflow: **Replacing a Module in The Grid** Replace one module in your patch with another module. Wann: When you want to swap out a module with a related one while maintaining its settings and connections. Schritte: 1. Drag the new module from the palette onto the center of the module you wish to replace. | 2. Alternatively, right-click on the module you wish to replace and select the new module '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Return Control to Arranger', content: 'Workflow: **Return Control to Arranger** Switch control of a track or all tracks back to the Arranger. Wann: When you want to resume manual control of a track or tracks. Schritte: 1. Click the track\'s Switch Playback to Arranger button to return control of a single track. | 2. Click the Global Switch Playback to Arranger button to return control of all tracks.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Routing Audio Signals', content: 'Workflow: **Routing Audio Signals** A workflow for routing audio signals using the Audio Receiver device. Wann: To redirect audio signals from one part of the project to another. Schritte: 1. Insert an Audio Receiver device on a track. | 2. Use the audio and/or note chooser menus to select the source of the audio signal. | 3. Route the signal to the desired destination within or outside of Bitwig '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Routing Note Signals', content: 'Workflow: **Routing Note Signals** A workflow for routing note signals using the Note Receiver device. Wann: To redirect MIDI note signals from one part of the project to another. Schritte: 1. Insert a Note Receiver device on a track. | 2. Use the audio and/or note chooser menus to select the source of the note signal. | 3. Route the signal to the desired destination within or outside of Bitwig St'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Saving a Browser Snapshot', content: 'Workflow: **Saving a Browser Snapshot** Save a snapshot of your current browser search configuration for later use. Wann: When you want to quickly return to a specific set of search filters. Schritte: 1. Click the magnifying glass icon in the search bar. | 2. Select \'Save Snapshot...\' from the options. | 3. Name your snapshot in the dialog that appears.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Saving a Smart Collection', content: 'Workflow: **Saving a Smart Collection** Save a dynamic set of filters as a smart collection for future use. Wann: When you want to create a reusable, dynamic collection of content based on specific criteria. Schritte: 1. Click the magnifying glass icon in the search bar. | 2. Select \'Save Smart Collection...\' from the options. | 3. Name your smart collection and choose a color in the dialog that a'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Searching for Grid Modules', content: 'Workflow: **Searching for Grid Modules** To quickly find specific modules in the Grid editor. Wann: When you need to locate a specific module or type of module. Schritte: 1. Click the search field in the top left of the Grid editor window. | 2. Start typing the name or description of the module you\'re looking for.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Selecting Tracks', content: 'Workflow: **Selecting Tracks** Selecting one or more tracks for editing or processing. Wann: When you need to edit, copy, or move tracks. Schritte: 1. Click on the track\'s header to select it. | 2. Use [UP ARROW] or [DOWN ARROW] to cycle through adjacent tracks if already selected. | 3. Hold [SHIFT] and click on the final track to select multiple contiguous tracks. | 4. Hold [CTRL] ([CMD] on Mac) '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Set Project Tempo to Match Audio', content: 'Workflow: **Set Project Tempo to Match Audio** Changes the project tempo to match the primary tempo of the selected audio. Wann: Use this workflow when you want the project tempo to align with the tempo of the selected audio clips. Schritte: 1. Select the audio clip whose tempo you want to match. | 2. Go to the Tempo menu and choose \'Set Project Tempo\'. | 3. The project tempo will adjust to match '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Set Time Signature and Tick', content: 'Workflow: **Set Time Signature and Tick** Configure the project\'s time signature and tick settings for precise rhythmic control. Wann: When you need to set a specific time signature and subdivision for your project. Schritte: 1. Open the Play menu and navigate to the Time Signature control. | 2. Set the numerator to define the number of beats per bar. | 3. Choose a common denominator (2, 4, 8, 16)'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Setting Send Source', content: 'Workflow: **Setting Send Source** Configure whether a send takes audio before or after the track\'s volume fader. Wann: To control how audio is routed from a track to an FX track based on the fader position. Schritte: 1. Right-click the send knob. | 2. Select the appropriate setting from the context menu: Pre, Post, or Auto.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Setting Up Audio Thru', content: 'Workflow: **Setting Up Audio Thru** Enable Audio Thru in the Note Grid to pass through audio signals to the output. Wann: When you need to maintain audio signals while adding processing or modulation. Schritte: 1. Open the Note Grid device in the Grid environment. | 2. Locate the \'Audio Thru\' parameter in the Inspector. | 3. Enable the \'Audio Thru\' parameter to pass through audio signals.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Setting Up Control Thru', content: 'Workflow: **Setting Up Control Thru** Enable Control Thru in the Note Grid to pass through non-note MIDI messages to the output. Wann: When you need to maintain control signals while adding processing or modulation. Schritte: 1. Open the Note Grid device in the Grid environment. | 2. Locate the \'Control Thru\' parameter in the Inspector. | 3. Enable the \'Control Thru\' parameter to pass through cont'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Setting Up Cue Monitoring', content: 'Workflow: **Setting Up Cue Monitoring** Configure cue signals for monitoring specific tracks or previewing content in the Browser. Wann: Useful during live performance or when preparing tracks for playback. Schritte: 1. Select the desired cue output in the Cue / Preview output menu. | 2. Enable the Solo as Cue button to route soloed tracks to the cue output. | 3. Adjust the Cue Level to set the de'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Setting Up Frequency Modulation', content: 'Workflow: **Setting Up Frequency Modulation** Configure modulation relationships between oscillators using the modulation matrix. Wann: To create FM synthesis patches with complex frequency interactions. Schritte: 1. Enable modulation for the source oscillator by toggling the modulation enable button. | 2. Set the modulation level using the global Mod(ulation) control. | 3. Adjust the modulation a'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Setting Up Note Thru', content: 'Workflow: **Setting Up Note Thru** Enable Note Thru in the Note Grid to pass through note signals to the output. Wann: When you need to maintain note signals while adding processing or modulation. Schritte: 1. Open the Note Grid device in the Grid environment. | 2. Locate the \'Note Thru\' parameter in the Inspector. | 3. Enable the \'Note Thru\' parameter to pass through note signals.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Setting Up Output Paths', content: 'Workflow: **Setting Up Output Paths** Configure audio output paths for different speaker setups or external hardware. Wann: When you need to route audio to specific speakers or effects units. Schritte: 1. Go to the Mix View. | 2. Create or select an output path (e.g., 5-inchers, 8-inchers, FX Unit 1). | 3. Assign the appropriate outputs (e.g., outputs 3 and 4 for 5-inchers). | 4. Set the role of t'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Setting Up Voice Stacking', content: 'Workflow: **Setting Up Voice Stacking** Configure voice stacking to layer multiple voices for each note played, enhancing sound complexity. Wann: When you want to create rich, layered sounds using multiple voices per note. Schritte: 1. Open the Inspector Panel of the selected device. | 2. Locate the \'Voices\' setting and set the number of voices available. | 3. Set the \'Voice Stacking\' parameter to'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Setting Up a Sample for Looping', content: 'Workflow: **Setting Up a Sample for Looping** Configure a sample to loop seamlessly within a specific section. Wann: When you want to create a repeating sound or rhythm from a sample. Schritte: 1. Load the desired audio file into the Sampler. | 2. Set the Play Start and Play End parameters to define the section of the sample you want to loop. | 3. Set the Loop Start and Loop End parameters to matc'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Shape an Envelope with ADSR', content: 'Workflow: **Shape an Envelope with ADSR** Use the ADSR envelope to control the amplitude or modulation of a sound. Wann: To create dynamic amplitude or modulation envelopes for a more expressive sound. Schritte: 1. Insert the ADSR device into the modulation or amplitude chain. | 2. Adjust the Attack, Decay, Sustain, and Release stages to shape the envelope. | 3. Select the desired model (A, R, or '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Shifting Clip Content', content: 'Workflow: **Shifting Clip Content** Shift the internal content of a clip without changing its position or length. Wann: When skipping parts of a clip without moving it in the timeline. Schritte: 1. Select the clip in the Arranger. | 2. Adjust the Offset parameter to shift the internal content.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Shifting a Crossfade', content: 'Workflow: **Shifting a Crossfade** Move an entire crossfade in time. Wann: When you need to align crossfades with specific timing or rhythmic elements. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the bottom of the crossfade. | 2. Click and drag backward or forward in time to shift the crossfade.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Shortening an Arranger Clip', content: 'Workflow: **Shortening an Arranger Clip** This workflow demonstrates how to shorten an Arranger clip by removing a portion of it. Wann: When you need to trim or edit the length of a clip in the Arranger Timeline. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the top right edge of the clip until a half-bracket cursor appears. | 2. Click and drag to the left to shorten the clip.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Sidechain Kompression', content: 'Workflow: **Sidechain Kompression** Kick → Bass Sidechain Ducking Schritte: 1. Compressor auf Ziel-Track laden | 2. Sidechain-Input: Kick-Track (manuell in Bitwig UI) | 3. Threshold: -20 dB | 4. Ratio: 6:1 | 5. Attack: 2 ms | 6. Release: 80 ms'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Slice Audio Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Slice Audio Clips** Divides audio clips into smaller segments for detailed editing. Wann: When you need to edit audio clips in smaller, manageable segments. Schritte: 1. Select the audio clip you want to slice. | 2. Go to the Slice / Fold > Slice In Place… function. | 3. Set the slicing interval (e.g., on Beat Grid, Onsets, or Beat Markers).'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Slicing Clips with the Knife Tool', content: 'Workflow: **Slicing Clips with the Knife Tool** Create multiple cuts in a clip, audio event, or note with a single gesture. Wann: When you need to divide a clip or event into smaller sections for editing or performance. Schritte: 1. Select the Knife tool. | 2. Hold [ALT], click at the position of the first cut, and drag to the position of the last cut.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Sliding Arranger Clip Content', content: 'Workflow: **Sliding Arranger Clip Content** A method to shift the content of one or multiple clips left or right in the Arranger Timeline Panel. Wann: When you want to shift the timing of clip content without altering the clip boundaries. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the top half of the waveform. | 2. Press [CTRL]-click ([CMD]+[ALT]-click on Mac) and drag horizontally to slide the content.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Sliding Launcher Clip Content', content: 'Workflow: **Sliding Launcher Clip Content** Shift the content of one or multiple clips left or right within the Clip Launcher while preserving their length. Wann: When you need to adjust the timing of clips without changing their duration. Schritte: 1. Mouse over the top half of the waveform. | 2. Press [ALT] (or [CMD]+[ALT] on Mac) and drag horizontally to slide the content left or right. | 3. Op'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Soloing Individual Voices in a Stack', content: 'Workflow: **Soloing Individual Voices in a Stack** Temporarily isolate and hear individual voices within a voice stack for detailed sound design. Wann: Useful when fine-tuning the modulation or sound of individual voices in a stack. Schritte: 1. Enable Voice Stacking in the device settings. | 2. Click the voice solo menu (S) that appears beside the Voice Stacking setting. | 3. Select the voice you'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Stop All Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Stop All Clips** Stop all clips on a track or globally. Wann: When you need to halt all clip playback immediately. Schritte: 1. Click the track\'s Stop All Clips button or a stop button within an empty slot to stop clips on a track. | 2. Click the Global Stop All Clips button to stop all clips globally.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Switching Between Arranger Timeline and Clip Launcher Panels', content: 'Workflow: **Switching Between Arranger Timeline and Clip Launcher Panels** Switch between the Arranger Timeline Panel and the Clip Launcher Panel in the Play View. Wann: When you need to switch between viewing and launching clips. Schritte: 1. Open the Play View in the Tablet display profile. | 2. Locate the view toggle above the single track header. | 3. Tap the toggle to switch between the Arran'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Switching Between Object and Time Selection', content: 'Workflow: **Switching Between Object and Time Selection** Toggle between object and time selection modes to edit clips and events in the Arranger. Wann: When editing clips and events with keyboard shortcuts for precise control. Schritte: 1. Choose \'Switch between Object and Time Selection\' from the Edit menu, or press [CTRL]+[T].'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Switching Modulator to Polyphonic Mode', content: 'Workflow: **Switching Modulator to Polyphonic Mode** Toggle a modulator between monophonic and polyphonic mode for more complex modulation. Wann: When you need individual modulation per note, such as with polyphonic synths or modulation effects. Schritte: 1. Right-click anywhere within an occupied modulator slot. | 2. Toggle the \'Per-Voice\' option to enable polyphonic mode.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Switching Tools in Timeline Panels', content: 'Workflow: **Switching Tools in Timeline Panels** Switch between different tools in timeline-based panels for precise editing. Wann: When you need to select, edit, or manipulate events in the timeline. Schritte: 1. Right-click within any timeline-based panel to access the tool palette. | 2. Use the appropriate tool for the task (e.g., Pointer tool for selecting events, Time Selection tool for time-'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Toggle All Modulations from a Source', content: 'Workflow: **Toggle All Modulations from a Source** Enable or disable all modulations from a single modulation source. Wann: To quickly bypass or enable all modulations from a specific source. Schritte: 1. Click [SHIFT] on the title of the modulator source in the Inspector Panel.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Toggle Modulation Bypass', content: 'Workflow: **Toggle Modulation Bypass** Bypass or enable a specific modulation routing. Wann: To temporarily disable a modulation without removing it. Schritte: 1. Click [SHIFT] on the modulation routing text in the Inspector Panel. | 2. Right-click the modulator routing button and [SHIFT]-click the modulation in the context menu.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Toggling Module Palette Visibility', content: 'Workflow: **Toggling Module Palette Visibility** To maximize editing space by hiding the module palette when it\'s not needed. Wann: When you need more space to work with modules and cables. Schritte: 1. Click the four-by-four table icon in the Grid editor. | 2. This toggles the visibility of the module palette and its search field.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Transpose Audio Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Transpose Audio Clips** Shifts the pitch of audio clips up or down by semitone or octave. Wann: When you need to transpose audio clips for tuning or creative purposes. Schritte: 1. Select the audio clip you want to transpose. | 2. Go to the Transpose menu. | 3. Choose the desired transposition (e.g., Semitone Up, Octave Down).'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Triggering Clips and Scenes', content: 'Workflow: **Triggering Clips and Scenes** Trigger individual clips or entire scenes using the play button in the Clip Launcher. Wann: When you need to play back clips or scenes during performance or arrangement. Schritte: 1. Click the play button of a clip to trigger it. | 2. Click the play button of a scene to trigger all clips within that scene. | 3. Use the Global Stop Clips button to stop all '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Triggering Clips with Launch Quantization', content: 'Workflow: **Triggering Clips with Launch Quantization** Set up clips to trigger in sync with the beat grid using Launch Quantization. Wann: When you want clips to align precisely with the project\'s tempo and beat grid. Schritte: 1. Open the Clip Launcher panel. | 2. Select a clip slot and open the Inspector Panel. | 3. Set the Launch Q(uantization) parameter to the desired value. | 4. Trigger the '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Triggering Launcher Clips Normally', content: 'Workflow: **Triggering Launcher Clips Normally** Trigger a Launcher clip by clicking the play button in its top left corner or using a controller pad mapped to the clip. Wann: When you want to play a specific clip using its Main launch behaviors. Schritte: 1. Click the play button in the top left corner of the clip. | 2. Alternatively, press the controller pad mapped to this clip.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Triggering Launcher Clips with Alternate Behavior', content: 'Workflow: **Triggering Launcher Clips with Alternate Behavior** Trigger a Launcher clip using its alternate (ALT) launch behaviors. Wann: When you want to use the alternate launch behaviors of a clip. Schritte: 1. Hold the [ALT] key and click the play button in the top left corner of the clip. | 2. Alternatively, hold the controller\'s [SHIFT] button and press the pad mapped to this clip.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Triggering a Scene', content: 'Workflow: **Triggering a Scene** Trigger all clips in a scene and stop clips for tracks that do not have a clip in the scene. Wann: When you want to play all clips in a scene and stop clips in tracks without a clip for the scene. Schritte: 1. Click the play button in the top left corner of the scene.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Granular Synthesis', content: 'Workflow: **Using Granular Synthesis** A workflow for using the Textures mode in the Sampler device to create granular-based sounds. Wann: Useful for creating atmospheric or textural sounds using granular synthesis. Schritte: 1. Load a sample into the Sampler device. | 2. Switch to the Textures mode in the play mode section. | 3. Adjust the Grain size parameter to control the size of the grains. |'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Keyboard Tracking for Pitch Control', content: 'Workflow: **Using Keyboard Tracking for Pitch Control** Configure the Sampler to adjust the pitch of the sample based on incoming notes. Wann: When you want to play the sample across different pitches using a MIDI keyboard. Schritte: 1. Load the desired audio file into the Sampler. | 2. Set the Keyboard Tracking parameter to 100% to enable full pitch bending. | 3. Set the Root Note and Cents Offse'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Modulators in The Grid', content: 'Workflow: **Using Modulators in The Grid** Route Grid signals as modulators to control parameters in real-time. Wann: To create dynamic modulation effects using complex Grid signals. Schritte: 1. Insert a Grid module that can act as a modulator (e.g., LFO, Envelope, or Steps Data sequencer). | 2. Connect the output of the Grid module to the modulation input of a target device or parameter. | 3. Ad'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Module Scopes', content: 'Workflow: **Using Module Scopes** Visualize signal levels at each input and output port of a selected module. Wann: When analyzing or troubleshooting signal flow in a patch. Schritte: 1. Select a module in the Grid editor. | 2. View the oscilloscope display in the Inspector Panel.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Pre-cords', content: 'Workflow: **Using Pre-cords** Use pre-cords in Grid patches to simplify common module connections. Wann: To avoid clutter and streamline patch creation by using pre-configured connections. Schritte: 1. Open the Grid patch in Bitwig Studio. | 2. Locate the pre-cord toggle near an in port. | 3. Enable the pre-cord toggle to connect the module to the same module buss.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Snap Settings for Arranger Clips', content: 'Workflow: **Using Snap Settings for Arranger Clips** This workflow explains how to use the snap settings to align clips with the beat grid or other clips in the timeline. Wann: When you want to align clips precisely with the beat grid or other clips in the Arranger Timeline. Schritte: 1. Click on the beat grid settings menu (the 1/16 indicator). | 2. Select the snap options under the \'Snap To\' hea'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Stack Spread for Modulation', content: 'Workflow: **Using Stack Spread for Modulation** Apply modulation to multiple voices in a stack using the Stack Spread device. Wann: Use when you want to create unique modulation effects across multiple voices. Schritte: 1. Enable Voice Stacking on the parent device. | 2. Open the Stack Spread device and select a spread mode (e.g., \'0 to 1\' or \'Flipped\'). | 3. Adjust the modulation level to control'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Sub Scenes in the Clip Launcher', content: 'Workflow: **Using Sub Scenes in the Clip Launcher** Use sub scenes to manage and trigger clips within group tracks in the Clip Launcher. Wann: When working with group tracks and needing to organize or trigger specific clips within them. Schritte: 1. Each group track has its own row of sub scenes. | 2. Sub scenes use color blocks to indicate which tracks have clips within that sub scene. | 3. Sub s'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Track Headers', content: 'Workflow: **Using Track Headers** Access and manage track settings, such as color, type, name, volume, mute, and solo states. Wann: When you need to quickly adjust track settings or monitor track levels during arrangement. Schritte: 1. Locate the track headers to the left of the Arrange area. | 2. Use the Track Color stripe to identify tracks visually. | 3. Use the Track Type icon to determine the'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using Wavetable Synthesis', content: 'Workflow: **Using Wavetable Synthesis** A workflow for using the Cycles mode in the Sampler device to create wavetable-based sounds. Wann: Useful for creating metallic or evolving sounds using wavetable synthesis. Schritte: 1. Load a WAV file with a \'clm\' chunk into the Sampler device. | 2. Switch to the Cycles mode in the play mode section. | 3. Adjust the Root key to set the appropriate size for'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Browser Search', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Browser Search** Efficiently search for and select content using the browser\'s search field and autocomplete suggestions. Wann: When you need to find specific audio sources, instruments, or effects quickly. Schritte: 1. Open the browser. | 2. Start typing in the search field. | 3. Use autocomplete suggestions by pressing [TAB] or clicking the blue button. | 4. If results are '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Chain Device', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Chain Device** Process audio signals with a series of effects while maintaining control over the balance between the original and processed signals. Wann: When you need to apply multiple effects in series to an audio signal and control the balance between the original and processed signals. Schritte: 1. Insert the Chain device into the audio signal path. | 2. Add effects to t'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Clip Launcher Panel', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Clip Launcher Panel** Understand and use the Clip Launcher Panel for improvisation and live performance. Wann: When you need to trigger clips spontaneously or for live performance. Schritte: 1. Access the Clip Launcher Panel either in the Arranger Timeline Panel or the Mixer Panel. | 2. Use the slots to house clips and trigger them as needed. | 3. Use the Stop Clips button to'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the FX Layer Device', content: 'Workflow: **Using the FX Layer Device** Apply multiple parallel effects to the same audio signal with independent control over each chain. Wann: When you need to apply multiple parallel effects to the same audio signal with independent control over each chain. Schritte: 1. Insert the FX Layer device into the audio signal path. | 2. Add multiple audio chains to the FX Layer device. | 3. Use the int'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the FX Selector Device', content: 'Workflow: **Using the FX Selector Device** Switch between different audio processing chains based on triggers or automation. Wann: When you need to switch between different audio processing chains based on triggers or automation. Schritte: 1. Insert the FX Selector device into the audio signal path. | 2. Add multiple audio chains to the FX Selector device. | 3. Use the Note/MIDI Source chooser to '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the File Kind Filter', content: 'Workflow: **Using the File Kind Filter** Access and use the File Kind filter to isolate results to specific file types. Wann: To narrow down search results to a specific file type, such as audio samples or device presets. Schritte: 1. Press [F] from most browser locations to alternate focus between the File Kind filter and the more specific File Type filter.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Inspector Panel', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Inspector Panel** To access and modify parameters of selected objects in the Arrange view. Wann: When you need to adjust track or device parameters quickly. Schritte: 1. Click the \'i\' icon in the window\'s footer to toggle the Inspector Panel visibility. | 2. Select an object in the Arrange view. | 3. The Inspector Panel will display relevant parameters for the selected object'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Instrument Layer Device', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Instrument Layer Device** Layer multiple instruments or sounds simultaneously with independent control over each. Wann: When you need to layer multiple instruments or sounds simultaneously with independent control over each. Schritte: 1. Insert the Instrument Layer device into the audio signal path. | 2. Add multiple instruments to the Instrument Layer device. | 3. Use the in'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Instrument Selector Device', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Instrument Selector Device** Switch between different instrument layers based on note input, MIDI controllers, or automation. Wann: When you need to switch between different instrument layers based on note input, MIDI controllers, or automation. Schritte: 1. Insert the Instrument Selector device into the audio signal path. | 2. Add multiple instruments and their associated de'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Location Filter', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Location Filter** Access and use the Location filter to specify where content is sourced from. Wann: To filter content based on its origin, such as local libraries, Bitwig Studio content, or installed packages. Schritte: 1. Press [L] from most browser locations to move focus to the Location filter. | 2. Click on top-level items to show results from that entire location, or un'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Mid-Side Split Device', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Mid-Side Split Device** Apply different processing to the center and stereo components of a stereo signal. Wann: When you need to apply different processing to the center and stereo components of a stereo signal. Schritte: 1. Insert the Mid-Side Split device into the audio signal path. | 2. Add effects to the mid and side chains independently.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Mix Parameter for Blending', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Mix Parameter for Blending** Blend original and processed signals using the Mix parameter on a device. Wann: When you want to control the balance between dry and wet signals in an effect. Schritte: 1. Locate the Mix parameter in the bottom right corner of the device. | 2. Adjust the Mix parameter to control the balance between dry and wet signals. | 3. Set to 100% for full we'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Multiband FX-2 Device', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Multiband FX-2 Device** Apply different effects to different frequency ranges of an audio signal. Wann: When you need to apply different effects to different frequency ranges of an audio signal. Schritte: 1. Insert the Multiband FX-2 device into the audio signal path. | 2. Adjust the split frequency to define the boundary between the two chains. | 3. Add effects to the low an'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Multiband FX-3 Device', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Multiband FX-3 Device** Apply different effects to three distinct frequency ranges of an audio signal. Wann: When you need to apply different effects to three distinct frequency ranges of an audio signal. Schritte: 1. Insert the Multiband FX-3 device into the audio signal path. | 2. Adjust the two split frequencies to define the boundaries between the three chains. | 3. Add e'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the On-Screen Keyboard for Note Entry', content: 'Workflow: **Using the On-Screen Keyboard for Note Entry** Use the On-Screen Keyboard Panel to play and create notes on a tablet. Wann: When entering notes directly on a tablet. Schritte: 1. Open the Play View in the Tablet display profile. | 2. Locate the On-Screen Keyboard Panel at the bottom of the view. | 3. Select a keyboard mode (Piano, Octaves, or Fourths). | 4. Use your finger or stylus to '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Using the Steps Device for Sequencing', content: 'Workflow: **Using the Steps Device for Sequencing** Create a rhythmic sequence using the Steps device with modulation and tempo sync. Wann: Use when designing rhythmic patterns with modulation control. Schritte: 1. Add the Steps device to the modulation chain. | 2. Set the step count and direction for the sequencer. | 3. Assign modulation sources to the steps for dynamic changes. | 4. Use the \'Tra'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Viewing Module Help', content: 'Workflow: **Viewing Module Help** Access detailed documentation for a module directly in the interface. Wann: When learning how to use a specific module or its parameters. Schritte: 1. Select the module in the current patch. | 2. Choose Show Help Item from the Module menu. | 3. Alternatively, click the Show Help button in the Inspector Panel or press [F1].'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Vowel Morphing', content: 'Workflow: **Vowel Morphing** Create smooth transitions between different vowel sounds Wann: When creating vocal-like effects or morphing between vowel sounds Schritte: 1. Open the Vowels device and select the desired vowel positions. | 2. Use the Vowel Blend knob to transition between the selected vowel positions. | 3. Adjust the Drive control to add warmth and saturation to the vowel sounds. | 4.'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Working with Meta Clips and Group Tracks', content: 'Workflow: **Working with Meta Clips and Group Tracks** Use meta clips to represent and manipulate clips within group tracks in the Arranger Timeline. Wann: When managing multiple tracks within a group track and needing to manipulate them as a single unit. Schritte: 1. Group tracks will have meta clips that summarize their enclosed tracks in the Arranger Timeline. | 2. If clips within the group tra'});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'Workflow:Zooming in the Arranger Timeline', content: 'Workflow: **Zooming in the Arranger Timeline** Adjust the zoom level to view and navigate the timeline more effectively. Wann: When you need to see more detail or get an overview of the timeline. Schritte: 1. Place the mouse in-line with the bar numbers inside the Beat Ruler. | 2. Click and hold the mouse button, dragging upward to zoom in or downward to zoom out. | 3. Alternatively, press [PLUS] '});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:"Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: "Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough** Hey, how\'s it going, my name is Chee, and we\'re about to dissect the latest Bitwig demo track, titled "Hey Now." Let\'s jump right into it. So, starting off with our kick. Actually we have created all of this in The Grid, and we have actually used the recent addition of the MSEG — multi-stage envelope generators, specifica', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V4mvT4Tbc3s', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:"Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: "Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough** here is the same thing, except we just threw an amp to give it a little more girth and more aggressive tone to it, because the second drop is a dubstep drop, so obviously we want things to be lifted up a little bit, so...we have that. As you can tell, it has sort of a boom-bap feel to it. That kind of summarizes all of th', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V4mvT4Tbc3s', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:"Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: "Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough** only difference we have here is that we have assigned the velocity of our rimshot straight to a bunch of different parameters, and that is for the sake of hit variation. So any time we\'re hitting the same note, but at different velocities, we\'re actually getting different hits. So as you can see here, if we actually fold ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V4mvT4Tbc3s', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:"Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: "Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough** saturation in the post-processing. What\'s really cool with the Saturator is that you can configure it right here and it works very similar to a Dynamics, so you can push the quiet and the loud levels and essentially waveshape. If you don\'t understand how any of these knobs work, you can click "Show Help"and it will guide ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V4mvT4Tbc3s', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:"Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough#4', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: "Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough** that, as you can hear, it\'s the same hat but it\'s doing different things. And yeah just helps us not have a cluttered project, consolidates the process a lot and that\'s pretty much it we\'re using E-Hat Bitwig\'s built-in plug-in. and then the rest here are just kind of recorded low shakers. And that is all safety pins shoo', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V4mvT4Tbc3s', chunk_index: 4});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:"Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough#5', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: "Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough** just low-passing. A lot of this is kind of unnecessary, I was just boosting some frequencies here to bring the sub a little more forward. We have some mid-side EQing and that was just solely just to get some stereo out of the sub, or low end. Some compression, some boosting yet again. And yeah, Multiband FX. So yeah as yo', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V4mvT4Tbc3s', chunk_index: 5});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:"Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough#6', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: "Hey Now" by Chee – Bitwig Studio Demo Song Walkthrough** think those are two notes that are playing on top of each other. Because it\'s an alarm, and most alarms are pretty atonal, and jarring, you know. And that\'s just got some Blur, some delay.There\'s no rules really when you\'re playing around with FX. And then we\'ve got the same thing going on Except we\'re doing the MPE thing', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V4mvT4Tbc3s', chunk_index: 6});
