MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:A Guide to Bitwig Studio 5\'s MSEGs#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: A Guide to Bitwig Studio 5\'s MSEGs** Introducing Bitwig Studio\'s New MSEGS. Multi-stage envelope generators are popular for creating unique recurring modulation patterns that you can draw and shape on a beat-synced canvas Bitwig Studio now has five different MSEGS. Each one is tailored to a particular use. There are new LFO and envelope modulators, Grid modules for a hand-drawn ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XgGHYV2AGdU', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:A Guide to Bitwig Studio 5\'s MSEGs#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: A Guide to Bitwig Studio 5\'s MSEGs** the grid lines where pressing comma (,) and period (.) will switch to double or half time — or make it ALT-comma and period, moving the beat grid up or down by a triplet. You can also right-click on the curve for some transform options. They let you flip, double, or mirror a curve, for instance. Curves is set up as an LFO or sequencer. Its en', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XgGHYV2AGdU', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:A Guide to Bitwig Studio 5\'s MSEGs#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: A Guide to Bitwig Studio 5\'s MSEGs** an envelope is destined for a Slopes waveform Or see which oscillator curve goes wild as a Waveshaper in Transfer And since Grid modules also power the Polymer synth, the Segments envelope and the Scrawl oscillator can now be swapped in there for direct sound control. Two modulators, five modules, each with free drawing and a complete library', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XgGHYV2AGdU', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers** Bitwig Studio 5.1 includes two new audio FX: Sweep and Filter+. Both devices bring all of The Grid\'s filter modules and waveshapers up to the track level for direct access. Now, here with Filter+, we see one slot for any of the 14 available waveshapers, one slot for one of the 10 filters, and then some additional ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BSc6Lza2YVY', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers** gets harder, it should push the shaper into more extreme territory" Now that we\'ve got a dynamic process, we can pretty clearly hear the difference between a simple gentle distortion and a clipper with a little bit of character. It\'s cleaned up a little bit. Or a hard clipper that just directly pushes the signal u', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BSc6Lza2YVY', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers** and then the waveshaper and out, blending towards a middle position, which will usually be the most generous position because this is parallel. The input signal\'s going directly to filter A and out, and also directly to the waveshaper, followed by filter B. So both filters are getting a crack at the original signa', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BSc6Lza2YVY', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers** modern ones with additional resolution, or even different filter configurations, from going from from more of a text-to-speech-type sound to more of a vocal synthesis-type tone In the Character category, there\'s a structure that\'s kind of similar here, which once you see multiple cutoffs, something interesting is ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BSc6Lza2YVY', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers#4', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: A Tour of Bitwig Studio 5.1\'s Audio FX, Filters and Waveshapers** with these various options, there are ways to go between them. If we started with Filter+ and we liked our filter, waveshaper and modulators, we could go ahead and convert that to a Sweep device, keeping them and adding filter A and the central controls on top, in case we\'d like something a bit more unique. Or not', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BSc6Lza2YVY', chunk_index: 4});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Automation Clips – New in Bitwig Studio 6#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Automation Clips – New in Bitwig Studio 6** Clips let you step aside from the details and think in musical blocks. Automation clips offer this same approach for automation. Alt-clicking beneath a note or audio clip creates a matching automation clip. Edits to the parent clip will also affect matching automation clips. If you want automation to be treated separately, just edit th', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=i4MMEsNbUww', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Automation Editing in Bitwig Studio 6#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Automation Editing in Bitwig Studio 6** Automation can be as important as notes or audio, and Bitwig Studio\'s improved workflows will elevate how you work with it. For starters, there are multiple ways to access automation. In addition to unfolding all lanes in the arranger, the Automation Mode keeps your layout in place, overlaying one parameter lane on each track. Whatever par', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5CQQu51ykO4', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Bitwig Connect 4/12 Feature Overview#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Bitwig Connect 4/12 Feature Overview** Hi, I\'m Lightbath, and this is Bitwig Connect 4/12. Bitwig Connect 4/12 is a 4-input, 12-output, class-compliant USB audio interface that can send and receive both audio and control voltage, allowing for integration with hardware synthesizers like this Eurorack modular. While any DC-coupled audio interface can be used with Bitwig Studio to ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_FSnMuA_2-Q', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Bitwig Connect 4/12 Feature Overview#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Bitwig Connect 4/12 Feature Overview** selected, you can press and hold In 1 or In 2 and turn the dial to set the direct monitoring level for that selected output. Lastly, you can hold In 1 or In 2 without turning the dial and after one second, the LED ring will display the current direct monitoring level. The three buttons to the right of the center dial are the Monitor Section', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_FSnMuA_2-Q', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Bitwig Connect 4/12 Feature Overview#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Bitwig Connect 4/12 Feature Overview** Play. The Bitwig button on the bottom-right can be used as a shift function to make these buttons control Bitwig\'s automation write, metronome, and loop. The top panel has two inputs and four outputs available as 3.5 mm jacks. This lets you connect Eurorack and other synths as audio or control signals. What\'s special about these connections', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_FSnMuA_2-Q', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Bitwig Connect 4/12 Feature Overview#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Bitwig Connect 4/12 Feature Overview** are these 6.35 mm jacks for balanced or unbalanced audio outs. This 6.35 mm jack is a stereo headphone output. These two 3.5 mm jacks are TRS MIDI in and out. The unit ships with two DIN adapters if that\'s how you roll. Lastly, we have the USB-C port for connecting to your computer and providing bus power. And because "safety first," a slot', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_FSnMuA_2-Q', chunk_index: 3});
