MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:EQ+ [Bitwig Studio]#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: EQ+ [Bitwig Studio]** Hello, and welcome back to Bitwig. Today we\'re taking a closer look at EQ+, the flagship equalizer of Bitwig Studio. Its tangible interface and pristine sound quality make EQ+ a flexible tool for mixing, mastering, and sound design in Bitwig Studio. Let\'s locate EQ+ in the pop-up browser and get started. The spectrum analyzer is already active in the backgr', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=COHi4GnpyYI', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:EQ+ [Bitwig Studio]#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: EQ+ [Bitwig Studio]** handy way of changing the Q factor in combination with the frequency while keeping the gain value constant instead. Another way to adjust Q and frequency together is the secondary handle with the double arrow which you\'ll find on any EQ band while you click and drag it you\'ll additionally hear this specific band in a solo mode this helps you to really focus', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=COHi4GnpyYI', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:EQ+ [Bitwig Studio]#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: EQ+ [Bitwig Studio]** all the tweaks and settings on your EQ+ here, without ever going back to the device panel at the bottom in fact you can also hide that to make the EQ display even larger otherwise an expanded view of the device panel shows a neat overview of all the eight EQ bands and their properties. A nice option of any expanded device view in Bitwig Studio is to undock ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=COHi4GnpyYI', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:EQ+ [Bitwig Studio]#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: EQ+ [Bitwig Studio]** since note messages are passed all the way down the device chain we could adjust global shift dynamically with the incoming notes. By adding a key track modulator here with the root note set to c3 and assigning it to the full range of the global shift, each note in this sequence will get its own individual treatment of EQ+, as the curve now adapts to the in', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=COHi4GnpyYI', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started With Bitwig Connect 4/12#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started With Bitwig Connect 4/12** Hi, I\'m Lightbath. Let\'s get started with Bitwig Connect 4/12. In this video, we\'ll go through the process of setting up Bitwig Connect to work with your computer, speakers, headphones, and external hardware. I\'ll demonstrate all the steps, so even if this is your first time using an audio interface, you\'ll have the tools you need to ge', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndQoO1Oi6kE', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started With Bitwig Connect 4/12#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started With Bitwig Connect 4/12** dial to add or subtract up to 12 decibels. Okay, let\'s open Bitwig Studio. Open the Settings by clicking the Bitwig logo at the top of the window and find the Audio tab. I\'ll use Apple\'s native audio driver. If you\'re on Windows, the best choice is ASIO. Connect 4/12 should be automatically selected, and Sample Rate and Block Size can r', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndQoO1Oi6kE', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started With Bitwig Connect 4/12#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started With Bitwig Connect 4/12** first turn on the Metronome with this handy button combo, and press Record to prepare for recording. Now, I\'ll press Play and get in the synth zone. And when I\'m done, I can press Stop. This is a great opportunity to use Scroll Mode to move the playhead around to different parts of the recording. I can do all of this without even using ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ndQoO1Oi6kE', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio** Greetings and hellos to the Bitwig community and beyond. In this video we\'ll be taking a look at audio editing in Bitwig Studio. The ability to edit audio is pretty standard on every DAW, but Bitwig Studio very cleverly integrates the most useful and necessary functions into its next generation workflow. It\'s designed to be very ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OqEHDZWhTPA', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio** file, By using the Knife tool, I can make slices and separate the clip into different pieces. However there\'s actually a faster way to do this, that doesn\'t require any switching of tools. When I move the cursor to the center of the audio file, the tool switches to the Time Selection tool. With the Time Selection tool, I can perf', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OqEHDZWhTPA', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio** And just to be completely crystal clear on the terminology – an audio file in this view is known as an Audio Event. So let\'s start with Audio Event expressions. All of the edits that we performed in the arranger timeline can be performed here with this, so we can adjust the Start as well as the End of an Audio Event. We can use t', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OqEHDZWhTPA', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio** able to go with audio events expression So when we\'re in stretch expressions you\'ll see that down at the bottom left and right hand corner of the audio event There\'s what\'s known as a beat marker. And if I scroll along the bottom of the audio event you\'ll see that beat marker appears and I can simply click and drag this to stretc', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OqEHDZWhTPA', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio#4', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio** at the onset markers the beat markers or on the beat grid at whatever division that I want. So I\'m going to slice it based on the onsets when I do this it dices up the audio and allows me to erase slices It will allow me to reorder and move slices around It\'ll allow me to duplicate slices and this is great for doing all types of ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OqEHDZWhTPA', chunk_index: 4});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio#5', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Audio Editing in Bitwig Studio** for pitch changes as well as Panning changes where I don\'t necessarily want a smooth curve all the way across. Now as you see when I draw these steps in the default value is 16th notes But let me go ahead and select all of these points. and delete them I can go down to the bottom and I can change my grid resolution. So I\'m going ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=OqEHDZWhTPA', chunk_index: 5});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Bitwig Studio Basics#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Bitwig Studio Basics** Hello, and welcome back. In the previous video, we showed you how to register your license and install Bitwig Studio. Now we are ready to explore some sounds. A click on the Bitwig logo here at the top takes you to the Dashboard, which is the central place in Bitwig Studio to access settings, sound content, user guides, and projects. You\'l', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vgIxeMVSQWw', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Bitwig Studio Basics#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Bitwig Studio Basics** classic way to start building your song. As you see, Bitwig makes it easy to get started, but let\'s go back to the Dashboard and take a look at the Help tab. Here you\'ll find online tutorials, as well as the Bitwig User Guide, and our short and crisp Getting Started Guide, which is there to make your next steps into Bitwig Studio as smooth', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vgIxeMVSQWw', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Bitwig Studio\'s Arranger and Clip Launcher#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Bitwig Studio\'s Arranger and Clip Launcher** Greetings and hellos to the Bitwig community and beyond. In this video, we\'ll be taking a look at Bitwig Studio\'s two sequencers. Each sequencer can work independently from the other, however the real power comes from when we use both sequencers together. And having two sequencers is extremely powerful because it allo', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vx20E5qvW6A', chunk_index: 0});
