MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Bitwig Studio\'s Arranger and Clip Launcher#4', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Bitwig Studio\'s Arranger and Clip Launcher** to a new blank scene And I have a complete phrase made out of the three different short ideas This is a very powerful very creative way that otherwise would have taken a bit of editing across the timeline But thanks to the way that the two sequencers work together this was a very easy task So as you\'ve seen having two', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vx20E5qvW6A', chunk_index: 4});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Bitwig Studio\'s Help View#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Bitwig Studio\'s Help View** Have you ever wondered about this Show Help button in the Inspector Panel of Bitwig? If I click on the Show Help button or simply press F1 on my keyboard, this brings up Bitwig\'s Interactive Help View. This is available for every single device in the DAW – so, every instrument, every audio effect, every note effect, every modulator, a', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S_nVhHf0WsQ', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Installing Bitwig Sound Content#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Installing Bitwig Sound Content** Hello and welcome back to Bitwig. Previously in this series, we went through the first launch of Bitwig Studio and installed the Essentials sound collection when asked. But there is much more factory content worth exploring. In the Dashboard you\'ll find the Packages tab. A sound content Package in Bitwig Studio contains any mix ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XPJuM6ATjNE', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Installing Bitwig Studio#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Installing Bitwig Studio** Welcome to Bitwig Studio. In this video, we\'ll show you how to download and install the application, as well as what to do when you open the program for the first time. First, go to Bitwig website to create your user account. On your user account page, you will find an overview of licenses you own. Click one of the download buttons to ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IgYdzHaQkZo', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Modulation Basics#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Modulation Basics** Bitwig Studio has a highly advanced and extremely flexible Unified Modulation System. It contains over 40 different modulators that allow you to control parameters at every level of the program, from devices and tracks to global settings. In this video, we\'ll provide a general introduction to the Unified Modulation System. We\'ll show you how ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sp-Nc90fyCw', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Modulation Basics#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Modulation Basics** filter to create a slight wobble effect by duplicating the LFO and mapping it to the cutoff as well. First we\'ll find a good speed and synchronize it to the project, and then we can dial in the amount and apply a slight fade-in. We can also add some punch with a pitch envelope. So let\'s add another ADSR, and we\'ll make it really short... ...t', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sp-Nc90fyCw', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Modulation Basics#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Modulation Basics** So let\'s give our Random modulator one last task: to randomize the octave of each note. Bitwig\'s instruments and Grid devices uniquely offer per-voice modulation. this This means modulators can treat each simultaneously played note individually, which creates a richer and truly polyphonic sound. Right-click on a modulator to enable per-voice ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sp-Nc90fyCw', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio** Greetings and hellos to the Bitwig community and beyond. In this video we\'ll be taking a look at the Note editor, and how we edit MIDI notes in MIDI clips. And keeping in stride with Bitwig\'s workflow, the Note editor is designed to be very fast and easy, and offers a number of shortcuts that never interrupt your flow. So let\'s ta', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4YgAttybw9Y', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio** we start playback in the middle of the note so for long sustaining notes like pads and chords the Note Chase will trigger the notes, even if we don\'t start from the very beginning of it. So once we have that set up, let\'s go ahead and record a new MIDI clip. I have a drum beat in this project, and i\'m going to use the Polymer to r', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4YgAttybw9Y', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio** hit the [Z] key, and that basically zooms in all the way so that i can see exactly what i\'m working on. Now just like with editing audio, we have a variety of different tools that we can use to perform different operations. But for many of those edits though the Pointer tool can do double or triple duty. With the Pointer tool I ca', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4YgAttybw9Y', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio** to enter different note sequences and I almost equate it to the way that a step sequencer would work. So let\'s just [Cmd+Z] to undo all of that. I can copy notes by clicking and holding the [Alt] or [Option] key, and dragging. I can also select a MIDI note, and hit the [Cmd+D] button, to duplicate it over and over again and in man', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4YgAttybw9Y', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio#4', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio** and this is going to allow us to correct the timing when we record live MIDI material in. So i\'m going to go ahead and hit this, and it brings up a dialog box, and it allows me to choose the grid resolution that we want to use. So we\'re going to choose 16th notes, and I can quantize at the Start and at the End, and if I choose the', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4YgAttybw9Y', chunk_index: 4});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio#5', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio** scale a range of notes as well, so if I select multiple notes and I go to the edge of the range, and I hold down the [Alt/Option] key, I can scale the entire range, using the left side as the anchor. And from the Note menu, I can also perform Legato. And what this is going to do – let me just select all of the notes – this is goin', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4YgAttybw9Y', chunk_index: 5});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio#6', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Note Editing in Bitwig Studio** is very useful for working with MPE material, where we\'re working with different MIDI channels in the same clip. "Pitch Class" is going to change the color of the clips depending on what the pitch is. And the final one is "Velocity", and again, this is going to change the color based on Velocity. So as you can see, the Note editor', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4YgAttybw9Y', chunk_index: 6});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio** Greetings and hellos to the Bitwig community and beyond In this video we\'ll be taking a look at recording audio from the outside world into Bitwig Studio. Probably the single most consistent feature that exists amongst all digital audio workstations is the ability to record audio and recording audio in Bitwig is very fast and v', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U5HxdO4piZk', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio** today 44,100 is going to be more than adequate Below this is our block size and this is probably one of the most important things to be aware of for when we\'re recording digital audio And we can think of this as our buffer setting and the idea with this is that as we lower the samples or block size we get a lower latency And th', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U5HxdO4piZk', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio** ahead and change that to mono in 2 And if I hit the arm button the guitar will come through when I start strumming on it But one thing I want to do first is I want to go to the play menu And this is where I can set up my pre-roll or count-off And I generally like to have 2-bar count off So let\'s go ahead and track the guitar in', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U5HxdO4piZk', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio** this to be the microphone input of my voice right now And I\'m going to arm this to record and now we see the signal coming in so I\'m just going to go ahead and hit the record button in the clip slot and just capture a little bit of audio Okay, so what I\'m gonna do now is I\'m going to create a new track And on this track what I\'', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U5HxdO4piZk', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio#4', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Getting Started: Recording Audio in Bitwig Studio** like a pluck Add a little bit more release And maybe what we\'ll do is we\'ll spruce it up with a reverb And let\'s just pull up a quick preset for this And maybe even follow it with a delay And let\'s hide the clip launcher And now what I can do is I can record this as a MIDI clip and create a melody line out of this recorded audi', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U5HxdO4piZk', chunk_index: 4});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:High-Level Editing in Bitwig Studio 6#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: High-Level Editing in Bitwig Studio 6** Bitwig\'s Launcher and Arranger contain clips and selecting a clip makes it editable in the Detail Editor panel below. Selecting a normal audio clip opens the Waveform Editor. Which is for doing edits and fades. Enable the expression overlay mode by clicking or pressing X. Audio events get an x-ray look so these event level automations, lik', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bOG3N9VMV3E', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:High-Level Editing in Bitwig Studio 6#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: High-Level Editing in Bitwig Studio 6** to paint a sequence, and once you release the mouse, new notes are selected for further action. Spraying out events is definitely fun, so keep in mind that dragging the Eraser tool can quickly tidy things up. Finally, clips work together. So selecting multiple clips lets you edit them side by side. When several audio clips are selected, ea', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bOG3N9VMV3E', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:How I Control Bitwig Studio With Bitwig Connect#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: How I Control Bitwig Studio With Bitwig Connect** Hi I am Leonard de Leonard and this is how I use Bitwig Connect as a controller. When I first started to use Bitwig Connect I was very interested about the fact that you can have like a shortcut on all the stuff you use ten times a minute like play, stop, record, automation and stuff like that. I really enjoy the fact that you ca', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2D6xMseDvvk', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:How I Use Bitwig Connect With Another Soundcard#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: How I Use Bitwig Connect With Another Soundcard** Hi, I\'m Leonard de Leonard and this is how I use Bitwig Connect in my studio with another sound card. I like Bitwig Connect because it\'s a good add-on on my studio. It\'s a shortcut on my setup. Sometimes it\'s nice to have something on the desk to record quickly one guitar pedal or just in front of the modular to add CV connection', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PI6hf5XK9Hw', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:How I Use Bitwig Connect as a CV interface#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: How I Use Bitwig Connect as a CV interface** I use Bitwig Connect with my Eurorack as a CV interface and also audio interface. Like that I can control VSTs with a CV from my modular, control my modular with like complex LFOs from Bitwig. Here I\'m using an LFO from the modular to modulate some plug-in in Bitwig, it\'s Pigments. I use the input interface to connect my LFO, so now I', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HI1IUwVLDow', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:How It’s Done in Bitwig: Unlocking the Rumble Trick#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: How It’s Done in Bitwig: Unlocking the Rumble Trick** Hey, I\'m Stimming. Welcome to another video of me showing you a trick in Bitwig. This time it\'s about the rumble. Why the rumble? Because in that certain circumstances, which everyone of you who\'s doing electronic music knows, everyone\'s who\'s doing music in general, where you\'re at that stage that there\'s already a lot going', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kyTe0JD5PZA', chunk_index: 0});
