MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** Welcome back to Bitwig Today\'s modular concept is mixing. Now, it\'s easy to think of sound design and synthesis as being something that happens primarily beneath the audio level, with various kinds of control signals and other things taking over. Now, that is true But there is also the regular audio processing side of things: Working with audio on the ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** solo button, called Exclusive Solo. If this is disabled, then now, that first click will let me hear multiple tracks at a time. So, again, if I go back to Exclusive, now the second I click somewhere else it will just turn off all the other solos. As you see in the window footer, Shift-click will always do the opposite behavior. So if you mostly like Ex', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#10', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** building this as a Grid patch, again I do have separate paths available to me, so if I click + before the instrument, it knows I want a note effect Maybe I want something to stutter a little bit and — ah Note Repeats might be the trick. I\'m going to give it its own life. But not everything... yes, I\'ve heard of this "Euclidean," I would like to try it ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 10});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** have this cue level available, because I find it pretty useful. But yeah. So right-clicking on any Solo button gives you direct access to both of these functions without having to go find them. And now, three topics that are pretty useful. One, in terms of mixing, is the idea of gain staging. And this might be the most obvious, but if I had a single tr', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** you end up in this place where you can have individual gain for each track. And it can be awfully handy, particularly with this sort of thing. I\'ve got a big heavy distortion device here... There we go. Now, if I were to adjust the gain before the effect, it has an enormous effect on the distortion Namely that it\'s not claiming as many victims, that it', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#4', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** Either of these gain expressions for audio or for notes. And then one last thing about the idea of gain is, I\'ve got a simple organ here. Very nice simple organ sound Let\'s get a bigger Spectrum...Now it\'s interesting that, if you look at the instruments we\'ve got, there tends to be an output control, and there tends to be a voice gain control. The dif', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 4});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#5', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** and that\'s pretty interesting... Now back to mono and poly for two seconds. By default the LFO is mono. It is not turned on poly. It\'s got the blue output, which means as I played one note at a time, it sounds great and it\'s tuned, but if I play different notes together, now we\'re going to get textural. Because that last note is tuning everything. If I', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 5});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#6', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** just kind of hearing something late, and some feedback being applied afterwards. If there was no feedback all we did was make the signal late. Oh now it\'s on time. So the Mix knob is really saying we want to balance the two of these things together, hear some of the dry, some of the wet and hear the overall effect. Now that can be pretty critical, so m', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 6});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#7', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** get to this idea of signal routing, now we\'re talking about part of a signal going one place and part of a signal going to another. We had this little sprinkle-y track here in the beginning. Obviously on a on a track by itself, a blend control, a wet/dry control, makes some sense. But an AUX send can also make sense. So over here on top of everything, ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 7});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#8', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** send, I could right-click on it. Just this channel going there. Okay, it was trusting the track, which is why the pre-fader switched things. Well I\'m just going to make this one send by itself pre-fader. Now I\'ve got the blue here, the yellow here. If I were to turn this up on a different track, it still stays yellow in the regular post-fader way Shift', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 8});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Mixing#9', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Mixing** a little bit Cool. Now let\'s think like a synthesizer. I\'d like to group these, because I want them controlled by the same volume, and okay let\'s just go straight to the project level. I think I\'d like something that can delay after a moment, so I have an envelope, which I found by typing in "delay." Yes let\'s have a pre-delay, let\'s say wait about two', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TM3cpL05qmY', chunk_index: 9});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Modular Concepts: Musical Maths [Bitwig Studio | The Grid]#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Modular Concepts: Musical Maths [Bitwig Studio | The Grid]** Hello, and welcome back to Bitwig, featuring The Grid. Today\'s modular concept is math. Now the reality is any patch that you look at any system is using a great deal of math whether its signals being added together by the mixer or even the multiplication of level adjustments and panning that\'s occurring there or the S', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2egzMCZ-N4E', chunk_index: 0});
