MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:On Microtuning [Bitwig Studio | Micro-Pitch Device]#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: On Microtuning [Bitwig Studio | Micro-Pitch Device]** with my hand, I don\'t have to be a piano player and I\'m getting something different, maybe inspiring even. Oh, and when I to go to the octave it\'s actually dropping the pitch much further down. Let\'s try playing two octaves worth of notes that\'ll be landing in different places but still parallel. Interesting, or even just shi', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Sm573jmEQs', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:On Microtuning [Bitwig Studio | Micro-Pitch Device]#4', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: On Microtuning [Bitwig Studio | Micro-Pitch Device]** well together. So, if I think about music and I let something go and maybe just start playing with two notes at a time, any two notes that I select should work well together. Then this is just a different kind of tonality. Instead of using microtonal in the normal way of "I need more possible notes" I\'m saying I need less and', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Sm573jmEQs', chunk_index: 4});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:One more Powersynth idea...#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: One more Powersynth idea...** Howdy. One more minute on powersynth ideas. And the thought today is, even with a basic synth sound, it\'s a good way to explore what seem like the tiny features of integrated synthesisers So here on Polysynth, this hidden little menu is actually masking a waveshaper that\'s incorporated with the filter. So if you try any waveshape mode, just remember', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=275_DEQRqbE', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Operators in Bitwig Studio 4#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Operators in Bitwig Studio 4** Hello everyone, welcome, thank you for watching. My name is Pat Cupo. I\'m a musician, I\'m a producer, and I\'m super, super excited to introduce you to the brand new Operators feature in Bitwig Studio. These are gonna take your note sequence and audio events to a brand new time and space that you probably wouldn\'t have thought possible. So I can\'t w', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wzGtsl-opXQ', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Operators in Bitwig Studio 4#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Operators in Bitwig Studio 4** try using the recurrence mode to determine when it will actually play. For example, I have it set up to come in every eight measures. Next, take a look at the dice icons on the note heads themselves. Those tell you how likely the notes will play on each loop with a clip. And this beat, I like how the open high hat will sometimes ring out because th', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wzGtsl-opXQ', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Operators in Bitwig Studio 4#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Operators in Bitwig Studio 4** its moody vibe. In addition to that, when I press the zero key on my keyboard, I activate lots of effects on the fill mode up above that\'s activating some new notes in the keys track while the lead part goes down an octave. Now, let\'s hear it all put together. Finally, the bass part. This is actually made from a bounce of a VST. So this means yes, ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wzGtsl-opXQ', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Operators in Bitwig Studio 4#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Operators in Bitwig Studio 4** new operators in Bitwig Studio. I for one know that I\'ll be using them in pretty much everything I do now. I\'m really looking forward to see what you guys do with it, the Bitwig community, to take these, have fun, experiment, and see what kind of crazy combinations of operators you can come up with. Take care, and I\'ll see you next time.', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wzGtsl-opXQ', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Precision Editing in Bitwig Studio 5.2#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Precision Editing in Bitwig Studio 5.2** Making music involves editing, and Bitwig Studio 5.2 brings precision editing under your fingers via the computer keyboard. Bitwig Studio has two concepts for selection: You can select objects on the timeline, such as clips, events, or automation points, or you can select time, which includes any events or partial events within that selec', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B2NKzmHki4U', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Precision Editing in Bitwig Studio 5.2#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Precision Editing in Bitwig Studio 5.2** this includes the arranger loop start and end as well as cue markers, when visible. But your musical events are available too, even when working in the Arranger. For note clips, each note start and end time is used. For audio, the left and right arrow keys move between onsets. You can hold Shift to make selections, and there are also new ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B2NKzmHki4U', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Richie Hawtin Controls Bitwig Studio Like A Drum Machine#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Richie Hawtin Controls Bitwig Studio Like A Drum Machine** Now we\'re going to look at a script I\'ve developed for the AKAI Fire. What\'s really important for me when I\'m using a computer is to have devices that interface to the computer and allow me to perform like it would be a hardware controller and actually try to stay away from looking at a screen. Bitwig has a very open app', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hyw3d4bD7ig', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Richie Hawtin Controls Bitwig Studio Like A Drum Machine#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Richie Hawtin Controls Bitwig Studio Like A Drum Machine** drum machine. So let\'s take the claps and copy them to the snares. Another interesting function is the ability to take an instrument and move it left or right, changing your rhythm and seeing maybe how you can make an alternate pattern. Let\'s move that clap and see if it sounds better somewhere else. And here as well is ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Hyw3d4bD7ig', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Richie Hawtin Controls Bitwig\'s Arpeggiator to Improvise Melodies#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Richie Hawtin Controls Bitwig\'s Arpeggiator to Improvise Melodies** I\'d like to show you one more script that I\'ve developed for a special controller from Argentina from Yaeltex. They\'re a small company that allows you to build your own controllers online and then configure exactly how you want So together I built the hardware with them and then developed a script very specifica', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3PzCyzEAp6I', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Richie Hawtin\'s Live Setup Using Bitwig Studio#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Richie Hawtin\'s Live Setup Using Bitwig Studio** Electronic music, whether it\'s in the studio or on stage, is at its best in performance mode. It should be playful, it should be fun, and it should enable you to kind of capture yourself and bring you a little bit more into the computer system Hi, I\'m Richie Hawtin, and today we\'re going to cover a new script I\'ve developed for th', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CEhpQH-8NDs', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Richie Hawtin\'s Live Setup Using Bitwig Studio#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Richie Hawtin\'s Live Setup Using Bitwig Studio** also have access to two FX sends and those are getting routed straight up into Bitwig So I have a reverb and a delay all being controlled from the K2s As a third FX send, we use QB headphones to be able to send back up to Bitwig for a flange-like effect. All the FX in Bitwig get routed back to the MODEL 1. Reverb on return 1, flan', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CEhpQH-8NDs', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Richie Hawtin\'s Live Setup Using Bitwig Studio#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Richie Hawtin\'s Live Setup Using Bitwig Studio** is really the heart of this script, because it allows me to do strange rhythms, polyrhythms and keep things rotating over the records I\'m playing to bring that kind of hypnotic state. So quickly with one touch we can go and start changing the loop length of course when you start to play with different loop planks it\'s very easy to', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CEhpQH-8NDs', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Richie Hawtin\'s Live Setup Using Bitwig Studio#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Richie Hawtin\'s Live Setup Using Bitwig Studio** is not one bar in different sections but specific individual clips and that allows us some really great features What we can do is select a clip that\'s not playing and program it in the background and then bring it in when it\'s ready Let\'s see how it works By pressing shift you can navigate to any of the clips without actually hav', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CEhpQH-8NDs', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:The Smallest Generative Patch in The Grid#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: The Smallest Generative Patch in The Grid** We open up the Polygrid, select an empty space, pull down the Voices to 0 Also insert two Trigger modules One is going to 9, the other one is going to 16 We\'re gonna use a Clock Quantizer Quantize one signal to the other and use trigger output here Also a Long Delay We\'re gonna connect this oscillator\'s output to the Long Delay and the', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tHhctmNHMio', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Understanding Bitwig Studio 5.2\'s New Compressor+#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Understanding Bitwig Studio 5.2\'s New Compressor+** Hey everyone, I\'m Pat Cupo, and I\'d like to introduce you to Compressor+, brand-new in Bitwig Studio 5.2. Compressor+ thinks about compression a little differently. With familiar controls alongside unusual ones, and a superior approach to analyzing audio, it\'s an all-in-one compressor for any use case. Plus, with various Charac', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6fSFzDL3GsY', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Understanding Bitwig Studio 5.2\'s New Compressor+#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Understanding Bitwig Studio 5.2\'s New Compressor+** than the low end. I\'ll add in a couple of delay pluses for texture, check the guitar in the mix and enable Compressor+ on the buss. As you heard, Compressor+ certainly plays nice with its new neighbors, while having a clear influence over the tone of single and grouped tracks. To the right of the display, there are additional c', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6fSFzDL3GsY', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Understanding Bitwig Studio 5.2\'s New Compressor+#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Understanding Bitwig Studio 5.2\'s New Compressor+** section and Inspector, you\'ll find the VCA Color modes, which apply tonal characteristics to the compressed signal, and the modes are: Clear, Prism, Transistor, and Saturate. The Inspector also includes four stereo independence modes and a stereo Independence amount for setting the distribution and amount of stereo separation. ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6fSFzDL3GsY', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Using External Audio FX with Bitwig Studio#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Using External Audio FX with Bitwig Studio** Hi. In this tutorial we will take a closer look at Bitwig Studio and external audio FX. Bitwig features an extensive collection of audio FX devices, and of course it can host all your favorite VST plug-ins as well, but it\'s just as easy to integrate your hardware FX into your projects. In this video, I will show you how. Before you co', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0UUesTtWWc', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Using External Audio FX with Bitwig Studio#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Using External Audio FX with Bitwig Studio** If I move the distortion pedal behind the reverb, it not only distorts the dry signal, but the reverb tail as well As with all devices in Bitwig Studio, you can save your HW FX device as a preset and load it to any other track or project later The distortion pedal I just added to the track serves as an insert effect. This means it is ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0UUesTtWWc', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Using External Audio FX with Bitwig Studio#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Using External Audio FX with Bitwig Studio** way You can refine the sound by placing additional insert FX in the FX track, including other external FX. Although some FX are typically used as inserts and others as sends, you can generally use them either way, and experimentation can lead to some surprising and interesting results. This concludes our introduction to Bitwig Studio ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0UUesTtWWc', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio** Hi In this tutorial, we will take a closer look at Bitwig Studio and external MIDI gear. I will show you how to connect your hardware synthesizers, synchronize drum machines and groove boxes, and control external gear with MIDI CC. We will also take a glimpse at Bitwig Studio\'s Note FX and modulators, which add powerful new features ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ASJ5v7-zVo', chunk_index: 0});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio#1', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio** you press a key you should hear and see it in your track Now you\'re all set to send notes and control messages to your synth and monitor and record audio, all conveniently in one track. In case your synth doesn\'t have any keys, connect the MIDI controller and activate it in the Settings under "Controllers." If your instrument support', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ASJ5v7-zVo', chunk_index: 1});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio#2', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio** change messages, or "MIDI CC," you can control up to 128 parameters of supported instruments via MIDI. Take a look at your synth\'s manual to see if it supports MIDI CC. If it does, you\'re in for a treat, because Bitwig features an extensive collection of powerful tools to enhance your synth\'s features. To set up MIDI CC, simply add t', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ASJ5v7-zVo', chunk_index: 2});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio#3', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio** to life and take your hardware synth to the next level. To learn more about modulators watch our in-depth tutorial. Note FX are devices that process incoming MIDI notes and alter them before they\'re sent to the external instrument. They are not audio FX, but MIDI processors, and can be useful to spice up both live performances and no', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ASJ5v7-zVo', chunk_index: 3});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio#4', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio** the Dashboard and open the synchronization settings Set sync method to "MIDI Clock" and select your hardware device as "MIDI Input." Now when I press Play on Analog Rytm, Bitwig Studio plays along in sync. For this to work, make sure your external gear is set up to send MIDI clock and transport signals To play and control devices in ', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ASJ5v7-zVo', chunk_index: 4});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio#5', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Using External MIDI Gear with Bitwig Studio** is no preset for T1 available, I add a generic keyboard controller and select T1 as input in case you\'re wondering I go with the keyboard controller preset because I only want to send notes from T1 and no MIDI CC, so it\'s sufficient if Bitwig treats it just like a keyboard. I head to synchronization settings, activate clock and start', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5ASJ5v7-zVo', chunk_index: 5});
MERGE (d:Document {source: 'YouTube:Voice Stacking in Bitwig Studio 5.1#0', content: '**Bitwig Tutorial: Voice Stacking in Bitwig Studio 5.1** Voice stacking is a creative sound design tool in Bitwig Studio And it is simple. Just set the number of voices that each note should trigger and then choose which parameters should have variety across each voice Each simple drag sets both the destination and the range of modulation applied With Bitwig 5.1, voice stacking is even more powerf', doc_type: 'youtube_transcript', video_url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ep_O2wd4U_c', chunk_index: 0});